WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.060
- When we made our decision
in December of 2017,

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.790
I kept track of some of those predictions.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:06.840
You're going to have to pay $5 per tweet,

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.970
you're not going to be
allowed to post on Instagram.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:11.910
One of my favorites, the
Senate Democratic Caucus

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.417
Twitter account said,
"The internet will work

00:00:14.417 --> 00:00:16.230
"one word at a time."

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.560
Or one of my favorites,

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.438
this is the end of the
internet as we know it.

00:00:20.438 --> 00:00:23.021
(upbeat music)

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.910
- I'm Dave Rubin, and this
is the "Rubin Report."

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:27.940
Make sure that you're subscribed

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.380
and click the notification bell.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.020
Otherwise, we're just talking
to ourselves over here.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.990
And joining me today is the chairman

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.690
of the Federal Communications
Commission, aka the FCC.

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:40.790
Ajit Pai, welcome to the "Rubin Report."

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.000
- Thanks so much for having me on, Dave.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.060
- I am thrilled to have you here,

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.030
because I don't know that
I've ever had a guest

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.900
that probably has more
power to do more things

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.480
relative to the thing that I do than you.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.380
So that's a lot of pressure, my friend.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.340
- A lot of pressure, but hopefully

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.610
I will be able to equip
myself well, we'll see.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:00:59.443
(laughing)

00:00:59.443 --> 00:01:00.276
- We shall see.

00:01:00.276 --> 00:01:02.136
Okay, before we do anything
else, there is a meme of you.

00:01:02.136 --> 00:01:04.520
- (laughing) So I hear, yes.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:08.370
- You are a meme'd person
because you have a giant mug,

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:12.500
a coffee mug, a Reese's
Pieces logo coffee mug.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.380
And this thing has caught
fire on the internet.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:15.770
How did this even come to pass?

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:16.940
Why do you have this mug?

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.860
- It's so random, so about 10 years ago,

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.110
I was up in Hershey, Pennsylvania,

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:21.440
with my then girlfriend, now wife,

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.420
and I love coffee, I love
Reese's peanut butter cups,

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.090
and I saw this giant mug, and I thought,

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.020
oh, this is the perfect mind
meld, and so I just bought it.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.350
At our FCC meetings,
sometimes they would drag on

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.820
for a while, and I'm an
inveterate coffee drinker,

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.200
so I just brought this mug
down to our monthly meetings,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.420
started drinking out of it,

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.010
and then John Oliver did this skit

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.670
about two and a half
years ago, mocked the mug,

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.010
and ever since then, I'm
always getting these emails,

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:48.045
tweets, and whatnot, "You and your mug."

00:01:48.045 --> 00:01:49.480
The mug was the innocent party,

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.170
so gonna be collateral damage.
- It's a big mug, too.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:52.700
It's also a giant mug.
- It is.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:53.600
I actually measured it out.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.240
It's six liquid cups.
- Wow.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.780
- So I go through about
two cups of those a day,

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.440
two mugs a day of coffee,
and so it's a fair amount

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.510
of caffeine that's
going through the veins.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.151
But it's kind of funny how it's
taken on a life of its own.

00:02:06.151 --> 00:02:07.660
- All right, well, you
kept the coffee mug.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.493
It's our coffee mug.

00:02:08.493 --> 00:02:11.180
We tried to buy you a Reese's
coffee mug for the show,

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.960
but we were on a time crunch,
so we couldn't pull it off.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.670
But...
- Ah, yes.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.394
- I have brought you Reese's pieces.

00:02:18.394 --> 00:02:19.490
- (laughing) Just in time for Halloween.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.780
- Just in time for Halloween,
so I'll just put that there,

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.930
and if you so choose to eat a Reese's

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.270
throughout the interview, feel free.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.010
- Well, if you don't mind.
- Have I given you coffee?

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.173
I don't know what you're drinking there.

00:02:28.173 --> 00:02:29.006
(laughing)

00:02:29.006 --> 00:02:31.200
- No, I could take it
intravenously, if I could.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.710
But no, I hope you don't
mind if I maybe take a break

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:35.320
every now and then and
just start munching on it.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.100
- That's fine, okay.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.140
There's a ton of stuff I wanna
talk to you about obviously.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.650
I've never been more
sure that either YouTube

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.710
is going to completely crush
this episode in the algorithm

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.520
or make sure that everyone sees it

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.010
to thus prove that they
don't mess round with things.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.010
So I wanna spend, obviously, a lot of time

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.670
talking about tech
regulation and all that,

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.330
and a little bit about your history

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:59.200
and what the FCC was set out
to do versus what it does now,

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.080
and all of those things.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.140
But sort of broadly to start,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.510
how much time do you
guys now have to spend

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.880
on just purely the tech
issues related to censorship

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.410
rather than just pretty much anything else

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.090
that the FCC has ever done?

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.360
- It's a decent amount of time,

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.390
not so much because we
directly regulate them.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.210
Under the current law
we don't have authority

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:22.200
to regulate some of the
Silicon Valley tech giants,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.200
but in part because we do
regulate, broadly speaking,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.730
the infrastructure of the internet,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.560
and issues like net neutrality, of course,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.470
have been at the fore
for a couple of years.

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:31.880
- Oh, we're gonna talk about that.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:32.713
We're still alive.
- (laughing) I had a feeling.

00:03:32.713 --> 00:03:33.775
- We're still here, man, I feel terrific.

00:03:33.775 --> 00:03:35.525
- Somehow the internet still works.

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:39.230
So we do monitor a lot of
these issues that have come up,

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.830
and it's fascinating how much
the conversation has changed.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.990
When I started talking
about this two years ago,

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.830
it was thought to be sort
of an outlier opinion

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.130
to go after some of the
Silicon Valley tech giants

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:49.160
for some of the policies

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.820
or the lack of transparency in particular,

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:52.990
and now you see members of both parties

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.230
in both houses of Congress,
people on the campaign trail,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.027
the average American consumer is saying,

00:03:57.027 --> 00:03:58.007
"What's going on here?

00:03:58.007 --> 00:03:59.747
"We want more insight
into how these companies

00:03:59.747 --> 00:04:00.620
"are doing business."

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.460
And so we spend a fair
amount of time on that.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:03.410
There are other issues though

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.600
that occupy the lion share of my time,

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.140
5G, closing the digital divide,
tacking unwanted robocalls,

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.960
doing those kinds of
bread and butter attacks

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.940
that the FCC has been entrusted with.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:18.040
- Right, okay, so let's
just back up fully.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.840
Just tell people that have no idea.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.790
They hear FCC, they're not
exactly sure what it means,

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:23.680
what it's supposed to do.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.620
What was the FCC designed
to do originally.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.520
- So it was designed in 1934

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:32.720
to regulate the radio airwaves
and the telephone system,

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.670
the primary means of
communication back then.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.640
Our statute is called the
Communications Act of 1934.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.380
That act has been updated over the years,

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.700
most recently in 1996 in substantial part.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.670
Now our jurisdiction has
grown over the years.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:49.620
It's not just radio and
TV and telephone now.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.010
Of course, we have jurisdiction
over satellite issues,

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:54.860
cable companies, to some
extent, internet infrastructure.

00:04:56.173 --> 00:04:57.500
We're composed of five commissioners,

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:00.030
each of which has to be
nominated by the president,

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:01.690
confirmed by the Senate.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:03.480
One commissioner is
designated as the chairman,

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.090
and that happens to be me right now.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.060
I'm very blessed to be in that position.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.610
There is a huge staff,
about 1,500 people or so,

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:13.650
to help us do all of the work
across these different areas.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.870
They do fantastic work,
from volunteering to serve

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:18.270
in Puerto Rico after Hurricane Maria

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.500
to going to tribal reservations
in the mountain West

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.670
to help get broadband to
these hard-to-serve areas.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:24.620
They are the eyes and
ears of the commission.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:26.850
I'm at the top of the
pyramid, so to speak,

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.220
but they really deserve all the credit

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:29.390
for the good work we do.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:32.140
- How'd you get involved in the FCC?

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:34.600
What made you the chairman of the FCC?

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.686
- Honestly, a complete accident.

00:05:36.686 --> 00:05:41.250
Honestly, I became a lawyer
after graduating from college,

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.520
much to my parents' chagrin,
I didn't go to medical school.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:45.220
But trying to live up to--

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.390
- [Dave] Is that tough for Indian parents?

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:48.323
- It was an interesting conversation

00:05:48.323 --> 00:05:49.637
when I told them I was going to law--

00:05:49.637 --> 00:05:50.660
- Ah, he's gonna be a
lawyer, not a doctor.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:52.050
That's like a big hit, right?

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.900
- I still remember my
graduation party after college.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.270
My parents organized a little
party for me in my hometown,

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.997
and one of my parents'
friends came and said,

00:05:59.997 --> 00:06:02.700
"Oh, it's okay, not everyone
can get into medical school."

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.510
It was like a nice little pat on the back.

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:06.180
- That's hilarious.
- It's sort of funny,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.230
every now and then, I try
to explain to my parents

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:10.200
what it is I do, and when I told them

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.110
I was gonna be nominated for this position

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.400
by President Obama back in 2011,

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.520
my mom's first three
questions outta the gate were,

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.700
is this a full-time job, does it pay,

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.890
and if it doesn't work out, can
you go back to the law firm?

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:23.860
She still wasn't quite sure
what it was that I did,

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.002
but I finally got across that it's like--

00:06:25.002 --> 00:06:25.835
- That's actually hilarious,

00:06:25.835 --> 00:06:26.950
you show her the letter from Obama,

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:29.010
and it's like, is this a full-time job?

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:30.850
- Yeah, I told her this is a real job,

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:32.537
and even then, for a while,
she wasn't sure what I did.

00:06:32.537 --> 00:06:34.740
But a couple of years ago, I
was in LA for some meetings

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.560
and was going to see Judge Judy,

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:38.030
because I'm a huge Judge Judy fan,

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.447
and I told my mom on a Friday,

00:06:39.447 --> 00:06:40.960
"Hey, I'm meeting Judge Judy on a Monday."

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.150
She canceled all of her
patients for Monday,

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:46.810
flew down to LA with me, and
she got to meet the judge.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:48.877
She never told me afterward,
but her friends told me,

00:06:48.877 --> 00:06:50.337
"Ajit, she's so proud of you."
- That's it.

00:06:50.337 --> 00:06:52.310
- You got her in to see Judge Judy.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:53.750
Not getting nominated by the president,

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:55.990
not closing the digital
divide, it's Judge Judy,

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:57.200
the highest court in the land,

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.430
getting her into that courtroom,
that was an accomplishment.

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:02.970
- At the end of the day, it's
still about parental approval.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.090
- Yeah, so I think the candle still burns.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:06.510
Hopefully, after this job is over,

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.530
I think she may want me
to go to medical school,

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.310
but for better or worse,
I'm in this role for now.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:14.720
- Yeah, okay, so as the guy in this role,

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:15.830
can you explain a little bit

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:18.330
about your just personal philosophy

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:20.640
when it comes to what should be regulated,

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.500
what should be completely
left to the market,

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.550
and where those two things kinda meet?

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:28.070
- Yeah, so I have some basic
principles that guide my work

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.880
across these different areas
that we've talked about.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.240
Fairly simple principles,
but I think they're important

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:34.790
for regulators to keep in mind.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:38.170
One is that markets, not
preemptive regulation,

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:40.280
have delivered far more value

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:42.560
for American consumers over time.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.450
And number two, the rule
of law really matters.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:48.190
Administrative agencies like
ours can't just make it up.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:49.770
The law has been set by Congress

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.950
and enacted by the president.

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:54.460
Ultimately, we can't make a decision

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:56.260
that doesn't have a grounding in law.

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:59.040
And third, and increasingly
importantly in this field,

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.910
we have to respect basic
principles of economics.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:03.410
Things might seem like great ideas,

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:05.750
but if the cost benefit
analysis doesn't work,

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.030
if the different economic principles

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:10.260
that apply to our work aren't respected,

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:13.250
ultimately, you'll have a
very elegant plan on paper

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:15.590
that ends up foundering
on the rocks of reality.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:17.840
So we try to respect
those three principles

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:20.150
in everything we do, and it's not easy.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:22.510
Oftentimes, you're going to
make a lot of people unhappy,

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:24.180
but our job at the end of the day

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:26.410
is to deliver value for
the American consumer,

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:28.700
even when or especially when
it might not be popular.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:30.770
- Does it strike you that your job

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:32.340
or anyone that's in government

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:33.900
that has anything to do with regulation

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:35.870
that the optics of the job

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:38.501
seem much harder now.
- Oh my god, yeah.

00:08:38.501 --> 00:08:39.530
- Where 30 years ago, it's like, okay,

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:41.080
you're regulating the airwaves,

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:44.560
there's a couple of channels,
we're on our way to cable,

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:46.900
but if you do something, like the pushback

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.200
is only gonna be a certain amount

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:49.680
because it's just how it is.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:51.670
There's only a limit, you
might get a letter in the mail

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:54.220
or something like that, or some
phone calls to your office,

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:57.110
where now everything that you guys do,

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:59.500
and almost anyone that's in
the public eye at any level,

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.060
it becomes like an absolute firestorm.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.763
It must complicate the
job at a certain level.

00:09:03.763 --> 00:09:06.000
- I think it does, and that's
actually a very good point.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.510
So I've gotten to know all
of my living predecessors,

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:10.880
save one, going all the
way back to Newt Minow,

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.810
who was President Kennedy's
first FCC chairman

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.480
from '61 to '63.
- Wow.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:15.637
- I had lunch with him
a couple of years ago

00:09:15.637 --> 00:09:17.680
and was talking about some of
the things we're working on

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:20.760
and how exposed the job was, social media

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.490
and cable news networks,
hovering stuff and all the rest.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:24.323
He stopped me and he said,

00:09:24.323 --> 00:09:25.910
"I don't know how I
could do this job now."

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:29.130
Because back in the '60s,
three major broadcast channels,

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.030
your local radio station
and your local newspaper,

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:32.100
and that was pretty much it.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:34.810
I mean, satellite hadn't
really taken off yet,

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:36.220
cable wasn't a real thing,

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:38.440
and so it's just a very different job now,

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:39.790
and that raises a lotta challenges.

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:42.800
Everything we do is
going to be scrutinized

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:46.110
and there's going to be a
lot of commentary about it.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:50.090
I think it's fair to say the
FCC is much better known now

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:52.030
than it's ever been known before.

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:53.550
That does have some positive impacts.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.450
I try to talk about the
positive things we do as well.

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:57.370
But there's no question that the exposure

00:09:57.370 --> 00:09:59.400
for this position, for
our agency generally,

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.800
has made it a lot more
challenging than it used to be.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:04.560
- How much is the sorta
changing technology

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.530
kind of effect what you do,

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:09.320
or sort of expose the way
things were done in the past?

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:10.660
So like right now, for example,

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:12.830
if we were doing this on television,

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:14.550
whether it was network or cable,

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.350
there's a lotta limitations
to what we could say,

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:19.350
the types of things we could
show, or things like that,

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.400
versus we're doing this on the internet.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:22.770
We're on YouTube and podcast,

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:24.860
so there are some limitations, right?

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:26.650
Like we can't show porn
or something like that,

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:29.100
but pretty much it's
the Wild West out here.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.160
So you guys have different
rules depending on the medium.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:35.780
How sorta complicated is that these days?

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:38.360
- You've put your finger on
one of the central problems

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.650
in communications policy, is
that Congress sets the law,

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:44.030
and that law reflects a
snapshot of the marketplace

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.330
in a moment in time, and that snapshot,

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:47.940
especially in the technology sector,

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:49.870
very quickly becomes yellowed with age.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:51.460
And so we hear constantly, for example,

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:53.367
from broadcast TV companies, saying,

00:10:53.367 --> 00:10:54.807
"Look, we're competing in the same space

00:10:54.807 --> 00:10:57.587
"with Google and Facebook
and other companies

00:10:57.587 --> 00:10:59.807
"who don't face the
same legacy regulations

00:10:59.807 --> 00:11:01.727
"that we have to deal with,
you need to change that."

00:11:01.727 --> 00:11:04.060
And different sectors of the
economy say the same thing.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:05.510
There's always the problem of government

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:07.560
trying to fit the square
peg of the marketplace

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.210
into the round hole of the law.

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:10.970
- So what do you day to those
guys when they say that?

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:12.480
'Cause that's a pretty
compelling argument, I think.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:14.140
I mean, sometimes now you watch,

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:16.390
like we have regulations on
what commercials could say

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:19.140
be on television versus the internet,

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:20.130
pretty much anything could be out there.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:23.140
- So the way I think about
it, the thing I told my team

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.970
from the beginning and I've
told them every day since,

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:27.940
is think about where the
marketplace is right now.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:29.900
How would you construct
a regulatory framework

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:32.150
to deal with the marketplace
as it stands today?

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:35.050
And to the maximum
extent permitted by law,

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:36.480
we should adjust our regulations,

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.520
modernize them to
reflect that marketplace.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.260
And I rely on one of my
favorite political philosophers,

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:42.390
and I know he's somebody you've drawn

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:44.748
a tremendous amount of
wisdom as well, Yoda,

00:11:44.748 --> 00:11:45.581
(Dave laughing)

00:11:45.581 --> 00:11:46.414
who in "The Empire Strikes Back,"

00:11:46.414 --> 00:11:47.247
when he's training Luke in Dagobah--

00:11:47.247 --> 00:11:48.390
- I thought you were
maybe going John Locke,

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:49.734
but I'm willing to work with this.

00:11:49.734 --> 00:11:50.780
- (laughing) No, much
more profound than that.

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:52.480
Private property, very important,

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:53.719
but Yoda, when he's
training Luke in Dagobah.

00:11:53.719 --> 00:11:54.860
- Okay, now you've really got me.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:56.680
- Yeah, and so at one point
when he's training Luke,

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:59.310
he says, "You must unlearn
what you have learned,"

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:01.240
and that is so true when
it comes to Washington.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:03.380
The regulatory impulse is so strong.

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:06.090
When you see an Uber or a Lyft,
oh that's a taxicab company,

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:08.580
let's slap all the
taxicab regulations on it,

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:10.550
Airbnb, oh, that's a hotel company,

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:12.520
same thing in the telecom sector, too.

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:16.000
Let's try to shoehorn all
of these legacy regulations

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:17.050
onto these new companies

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:19.370
and to maintain those legacy regulations

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:20.810
on the established companies.

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:23.530
My goal is pretty simple, to
just modernize our regulations

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:25.040
to reflect marketplace we're in.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:26.210
Unlearn what you have learned

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:27.650
when it comes to telecom policy

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:30.070
and you'll unleash all kinds
of innovation and investment

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:31.570
that ultimately benefits consumers.

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.570
We don't wanna disincentivize companies

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:35.260
from competing and innovating,

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:39.270
because ultimately the consumer welfare

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:40.730
is what is at stake here.

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:42.170
- More importantly than any of this,

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:45.010
where are you at with the new "Star Wars"?

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:49.240
- Yeah, I haven't really been a big fan,

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:53.740
except for, I will say, I
thought "Force Awakens" was okay.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.304
- Yes, "Force Awakens" was fine.

00:12:55.304 --> 00:12:58.120
- But I thought "Rogue
One," that was awesome!

00:12:58.120 --> 00:12:59.530
- I need more force.
- Really?

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:00.373
- Yeah, without the force--

00:13:00.373 --> 00:13:01.740
- That ending scene though was incredible.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:02.870
- Yeah, no, the ending was amazing.

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:05.230
- Because then it really
feeds into that first scene

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:06.910
in the "Episode IV."

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:08.760
But no, "Force Awakens"
was pretty good, I thought.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:11.070
- You give me two hours
of the last five minutes

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:12.930
of "Rogue One," two hours
of just an onslaught

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:14.105
and then I'm thrilled, but all right.

00:13:14.105 --> 00:13:14.938
(laughing)

00:13:14.938 --> 00:13:15.790
Let's not get lost there, 'cause I sense

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:18.490
we could probably do that
for a couple of hours.

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:20.250
So a lot of this is sort of just fighting

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.460
the Washington machine, right?
- Yeah.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:25.060
- The machine itself is designed

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:26.880
to create regulation, right?

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:28.870
So they see something like Uber come out,

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:31.480
and now everyone loves Uber
and it's changing the business,

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:33.100
but now you've got all these interests

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:36.280
with the taxi companies,
and then suddenly you then

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:41.280
have to fight the impulse
to basically crush them too.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:43.910
- That's a pretty amazing
position to be in.

00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:44.920
- It's very difficult,

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:48.450
and there are two very
powerful forces in Washington.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:51.130
One is regulatory inertia,
that it's very easy

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:52.460
to maintain the rules on the books

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:53.600
because they've always been on the books

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:55.570
and they should always
therefore be on the books.

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:58.190
But the other one is some
of the entrenched interest

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:00.330
that say, look, no matter,
any regulatory change

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.100
is going to disadvantage somebody,

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.720
and so they will complain.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:05.110
Those people who are affected

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:06.690
will have a disproportionate voice.

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:07.890
They'll be very loud about it.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.210
And so that's part of
the challenge as well,

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:11.990
to try to have the longterm value

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:14.050
of a greater number of people in mind

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:15.070
when we make our decisions.

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:16.390
- So let's talk about that a little bit,

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:18.640
the lobbying situation.

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:22.070
How much of the influence that hits you

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:25.870
is lobbyists from either
telecommunications companies

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:29.300
or free speech organizations
or whatever it might be?

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:33.380
- Yeah, every sector I
think has a grievance

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:35.800
to share with the
agency, but our goal is--

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.190
- Can you explain a little
bit about how it works?

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:39.257
So you're in your office,
your assistant says to you,

00:14:39.257 --> 00:14:40.370
"You got a couple of meetings today,"

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:42.180
and now you know some
lobbyists are coming in.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:44.280
What is the process actually like?

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:46.010
- So yeah, I have a lot
of meetings every day

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:47.447
that I'm in the office in Washington,

00:14:47.447 --> 00:14:50.520
and what often happens is
that people sit on my couch,

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:51.527
very much like we're sitting here,

00:14:51.527 --> 00:14:54.367
and they'll tell me, "Ajit,
you're doing a great job

00:14:54.367 --> 00:14:56.437
"on this and that, and this and that.

00:14:56.437 --> 00:14:57.697
"We are all in favor of this.

00:14:57.697 --> 00:15:00.127
"We are big free market advocates.

00:15:00.127 --> 00:15:01.399
"On this particular issue--"

00:15:01.399 --> 00:15:02.515
- [Dave] (laughing)
There's a but go in there.

00:15:02.515 --> 00:15:03.348
- "There's one little tweak,"

00:15:03.348 --> 00:15:05.110
and it's amazing how
many times that happens.

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:08.500
I did a round table
recently out on the road,

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:09.880
and it was striking.

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:12.130
One of the biggest
innovations we've introduced,

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:13.880
we have a $10 billion subsidy fund,

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:15.540
known as the Universal Service Fund.

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:17.250
Typically this consisted of cutting checks

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:19.597
to rural telephone companies
and saying, "Vaya con dios,

00:15:19.597 --> 00:15:22.280
"we hope you build broadband
networks with this funding."

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:23.900
One of the major innovations I introduced

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:25.950
about two years ago when
I just got on board was,

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:28.360
instead of just cutting a
check, let's have an auction,

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:30.360
a reverse auction, where
all kinds of companies

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:32.640
using different
technologies have to compete

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:35.280
for this funding and there's
a downward pressure on prices,

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:37.780
it's technologically neutral,
it saves the taxpayer money,

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:41.200
yada yada, much more
efficient serving the cause.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:42.667
I was in this round table and they said,

00:15:42.667 --> 00:15:44.187
"We love all the stuff you're doing

00:15:44.187 --> 00:15:45.937
"on the Universal Service Fund.

00:15:45.937 --> 00:15:48.457
"It might be helpful though to exempt us

00:15:48.457 --> 00:15:51.420
"from the auction mechanism
because it'd just be..."

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:53.540
Look, I understand from
their business perspective,

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:55.720
it makes sense, but my goal can't be

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:57.580
to vindicate any private interest.

00:15:57.580 --> 00:15:59.290
It is not to vindicate
any private interest,

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:01.020
it's to uphold the public interest,

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:03.530
and that's going to make
people unhappy in many cases

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:06.820
because many business
plans might not survive

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:08.690
or might not thrive in an environment

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.760
where that kind of competition
is embraced by the regulator.

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.270
- So let's back up a little
bit to your philosophy again,

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:15.880
which basically you're
saying free markets,

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:18.060
you want them to do as much as possible,

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:19.520
and yet you're the guy that has to come in

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:22.230
and give some regulation
around the public good.

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:23.783
What do you think would happen?

00:16:25.082 --> 00:16:27.500
This is like sorta the
purist libertarian approach,

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:28.380
what do you think would happen

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:30.820
if just basically the
regulations just all disappeared

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:32.480
and just the free market just was allowed

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:34.440
to do whatever it wants

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:36.800
related to all of these
issues around communication?

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:37.890
What do you think

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:39.279
actually would happen?
- That's a good question.

00:16:39.279 --> 00:16:40.630
That's a very good question.

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:42.897
- By the way, I'm gonna move
this because otherwise--

00:16:42.897 --> 00:16:44.442
- (laughing) I know, I'm
very tempted, I gotta say.

00:16:44.442 --> 00:16:46.050
- I realize that if we
leave this the entire time,

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:47.380
the amount of memes that will come out,

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:49.020
that you had an hour long conversation

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:50.720
with a Reese's bar on the table.

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:51.710
- Oh my god.
- So I'm just gonna...

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:53.667
- It's become the MacGuffin already

00:16:53.667 --> 00:16:54.500
in the interview.
- (laughing) Yeah, exactly.

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:55.337
- For those film duffs out there.

00:16:55.337 --> 00:16:56.400
- You can have it later.

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:57.500
- You promise?

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:58.730
- Well, let's just see how it finishes up.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:00.311
- Okay, all right, we'll see how it goes.

00:17:00.311 --> 00:17:02.010
But your question was about

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:03.280
the lack of regulation altogether,

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:05.350
and I'm not one who says necessarily

00:17:05.350 --> 00:17:06.630
that we need to scrap everything.

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:09.190
For example, in terms of
setting spectrum policy,

00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:10.620
it's important for us to coordinate

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:14.100
among different government
agencies and the private sector,

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:16.850
but I do think it would be
an interesting experiment

00:17:16.850 --> 00:17:17.683
in some of these areas

00:17:17.683 --> 00:17:19.030
if the government got out of the business.

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:20.880
I'll give you one example.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:24.080
Back in 1975, one of my
predecessors instituted a rule

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:26.710
prohibiting newspapers and broadcast,

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:28.670
TV and radio stations, from combining,

00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:32.700
because that was seen to be
detrimental to competition.

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:34.750
Does anyone really think in 2019

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:38.230
that local newspapers
or local radio stations

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:40.390
are the dominant force
in any particular market,

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:41.730
especially some of the smaller markets?

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:42.920
I don't really think so.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:45.100
So if we got rid of regulations there,

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.030
I don't think it would lead
to all this consolidation.

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:49.340
To the contrary, it would
enable them to compete

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:50.754
with the Googles and the Facebooks

00:17:50.754 --> 00:17:52.470
and the Pandoras and Spotifys and others

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:55.130
who are out there
distribution information.

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:57.270
To me at least I think it's an
interesting thought exercise,

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:59.150
and as that denominator,
so to speak, grows,

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:01.960
as more people start to
compete in the same space,

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:03.050
I think it's important for government

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:04.720
to have an intellectually
honest assessment

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:06.070
of what competition is.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:07.570
That denominator is much broader

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:09.190
than broadcast radio, for example.

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:12.010
It's all those streaming
services and podcasts

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:14.370
and all the rest of it that people...

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:15.730
People just wanna consume information.

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:18.520
Information, honestly, is the
denominator in many cases.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.650
- So I think there's
probably a certain amount

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:21.707
of people listening to this going,

00:18:21.707 --> 00:18:23.347
"All right, he's down
with the free markets,

00:18:23.347 --> 00:18:26.187
"he's trying to use the
light touch when possible.

00:18:26.187 --> 00:18:28.767
"How was this guy nominated by Obama?

00:18:28.767 --> 00:18:31.237
"This sounds more like say a conservative

00:18:31.237 --> 00:18:33.000
"or a Republican approach?"

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:34.210
Can you talk about that a little bit?

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:36.790
- Yeah, so traditionally commissioners

00:18:36.790 --> 00:18:39.839
who are in the minority, so the
FCC I should have backed up,

00:18:39.839 --> 00:18:41.850
the five commissioners I mentioned,

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:43.950
three of whom are of
the president's party,

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:47.630
two are of the minority
party, the party out of power.

00:18:47.630 --> 00:18:50.620
So I was nominated in
2011, confirmed in 2012,

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:52.280
for one of the minority party seats,

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:54.680
and by tradition going
back a couple of decades,

00:18:56.510 --> 00:18:59.110
the Senate leader of
the party out of power

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:00.690
makes a recommendation to the White House

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:02.420
on who those minority
commissioners should be.

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:04.680
And so in my case, Senator
McConnell recommended

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:07.980
to President Obama, "We
recommend Ajit for this slot,"

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:08.964
and somehow I fell through the cracks.

00:19:08.964 --> 00:19:10.180
- So the president still
has to say it's okay,

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.605
even though you're the ones
getting the minority slot.

00:19:12.605 --> 00:19:13.700
That's kinda interesting.
- Right.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:15.040
So it's a very interesting process.

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:15.873
- So you're saying he still has the power

00:19:15.873 --> 00:19:17.670
to stop that minority person from coming?

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:19.400
- Theoretically, yes, and so fortunately,

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:20.430
I fell through the cracks.

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:23.140
I had good friends on
both sides of the aisle

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:25.570
and confirmed me unanimously,
so it wasn't an issue.

00:19:25.570 --> 00:19:28.030
But it's a very interesting
model of governance,

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:30.590
and I think a lot of the
cabinet departments, of course,

00:19:30.590 --> 00:19:31.810
which is just that of a secretary,

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:32.810
have a very different model,

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:35.680
where it's more of a single
person in charge of the agency.

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:40.420
- So how much then of
your job is coordinating

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:41.583
with the president?

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:44.600
From what I can understand,
it sounds like Trump

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:49.600
is probably more in line with
your philosophy around this,

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:51.580
but does that even matter that much?

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:52.630
Did you see a big difference

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:54.530
from when Obama was there to now?

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:55.500
- So that's an interesting point.

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.300
We are actually an independent agency,

00:19:57.300 --> 00:19:59.260
so once we are confirmed by the Senate,

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:00.240
we're off to the races.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:01.470
We don't answer to anybody.

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:03.290
We're independent from the administration,

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:05.440
independent from Congress,
although of course we respect--

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:07.110
- I assume they can create
headaches for you though.

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:09.050
- Oh, absolutely, and
there's certain issues,

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:10.960
especially when it comes
to security, for example,

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:12.830
or some spectrum issues
where we have to coordinate

00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:14.110
with say the Department of Defense

00:20:14.110 --> 00:20:15.320
or the National Security Council

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:17.580
where we do work with White House

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:19.360
and administration agencies all the time.

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:21.790
But one of the things that
the president-elect told me

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:24.757
when I interviewed for the
job in January 2017 was,

00:20:24.757 --> 00:20:26.367
"I'm giving you this job because I believe

00:20:26.367 --> 00:20:28.567
"you're going to do terrific things.

00:20:28.567 --> 00:20:31.207
"Do the job, I'm trusting
you with this responsibility

00:20:31.207 --> 00:20:33.557
"because I know you'll
execute as you see fit,

00:20:33.557 --> 00:20:35.570
"and we'll back you up 100% of the way."

00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:38.420
And so that's been largely
what it's all been about.

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:40.470
We've been pursuing a lot
of different initiatives,

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:42.730
and they have been supportive,

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:44.040
especially on things like 5G,

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:45.540
where we think it's a national priority,

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:48.300
not just an FCC priority,
for America to lead the world

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:49.570
in this next generation technology.

00:20:49.570 --> 00:20:50.880
- So what's happening with 5G right now?

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:54.900
Because I think now in LA in
certain spots, I am seeing 5G.

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:56.020
It just pops up on my phone.

00:20:56.020 --> 00:20:57.930
And I remember I was traveling last year

00:20:57.930 --> 00:20:59.500
that certain cities I would get it.

00:20:59.500 --> 00:21:01.410
And I'd be like, whoa, I didn't
even know this was a thing.

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:02.920
So how does that actually work

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:05.580
when you guys are trying to
build a nationwide network?

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:08.160
- Yeah, so 5G is going to be tremendous,

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:10.830
100 times faster speeds than
4G, if not quicker than that,

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:13.840
much lower latency, a quicker
responsiveness of the network,

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:16.690
and also all kinds of applications
we can't even dream of.

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:18.170
So we're just in the
early stages right now.

00:21:18.170 --> 00:21:20.380
The wireless carriers are
doing some deployments

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:22.180
in major cities, including Los Angeles,

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:24.470
and we're hopeful that's going
to ramp up in the future.

00:21:24.470 --> 00:21:26.380
I think we're gonna have
92 commercial deployments

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:28.420
across the country by
the end of this year.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:29.700
Many more are coming.

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:32.090
And so our goal is to just put
the building blocks in place

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:33.360
for companies to innovate.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:34.320
That involves spectrum.

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:35.310
We're getting a lot more spectrum

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:36.950
in the commercial marketplace,

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:38.400
held a big auction in January,

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:40.040
another one finished up in May.

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:41.380
The biggest auction in American history

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:43.160
is going to be on December 10th.

00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:44.840
This year alone, we're
gonna push more spectrum

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.680
in the commercial marketplace

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:48.500
than is held by every mobile
broadband provider combined

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:51.490
when it came to office,
so huge influx of supply.

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:52.720
On the infrastructure side, too,

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:55.570
we're modernizing our rules
to recognize that small cells,

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:57.280
essentially infrastructure
that's no bigger

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.360
than say that basketball
shouldn't have to jump

00:21:59.360 --> 00:22:00.590
through the same regulatory hoops

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:02.320
as a 200-foot cell tower.

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:03.910
And that's the kind of
thing that has real pop

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:05.420
here in LA in particular.

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:07.920
Just yesterday morning,
I was visiting a site

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:09.926
where they're deploying these small cells

00:22:09.926 --> 00:22:10.840
in a lower income neighborhood,

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:12.720
thanks in part to the
regulatory streamlining

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:14.260
that we've done and the city of LA,

00:22:14.260 --> 00:22:16.381
believe it or not, has done.

00:22:16.381 --> 00:22:17.600
- They're helping you?

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:18.433
The city of LA is helping you?

00:22:18.433 --> 00:22:19.550
- I've gotta say, Mayor Garcetti

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:21.150
has been a terrific partner on this issue.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:24.190
Other cities have raised
a few more roadblocks,

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:25.860
but those cities that
are forward thinking,

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.350
that want their citizens
to benefit from 5G,

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:30.130
are adopting this more modern approach

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:31.520
to infrastructure deployment.

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.540
So overall I think the United
States is poised to lead,

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:36.660
but there are other countries
too, particularly China,

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:38.340
that would love to seize
the lead for themselves.

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:40.460
And so we're in an implicit
competition I think

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:42.130
with many countries and
regions around the world.

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:43.230
- Yeah, so right before we started,

00:22:43.230 --> 00:22:44.300
we talked about this issue

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:47.160
with how China deals with
regulation differently,

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:49.083
and there are strengths
and weaknesses to it.

00:22:49.083 --> 00:22:50.270
Can you explain that a little bit?

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:52.550
'Cause I think it's a
really fascinating flip

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:54.540
on the way we talk about
a lot of these issues.

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:57.880
- It is, so China of
course is not a democracy.

00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:01.180
Beijing can dictate not
just what policy priorities

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:02.230
the government should follow,

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:04.580
but also require all provinces,

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:06.450
all localities to fall into line,

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:08.380
and so of course since they don't observe

00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:10.060
the same democratic nicety as we do,

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:11.620
in this case, they can simply fiat

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:13.510
we want to lead the world in 5G.

00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:15.010
That means we're going
to pick the companies

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:16.510
that create the equipment
and subsidize them,

00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:18.850
we're going to make sure
that our wireless carriers

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:20.510
can deploy at scale and
they don't have to jump

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:22.080
through these regulatory hoops,

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:23.760
and we are otherwise going to take

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:25.810
the steps necessary to preempt spectrum,

00:23:25.810 --> 00:23:28.140
do the other things
necessary for 5G success.

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:30.410
- So that kinda sounds
good at some level, right?

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:33.640
- Right, but there are
of course some concerns

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:35.610
for those who share democratic values,

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:36.970
such as the United States.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:38.840
Comparatively here, we have not just

00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:41.320
federal regulatory
approval that's required,

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:42.890
state and local regulatory approval

00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:44.070
that's required in many cases,

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:46.460
but also under a longstanding system

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:48.480
that the FCC set up many years ago,

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:51.920
any one of the 573
federally-recognized Indian tribes

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:53.910
also can have a bite of
the regulatory apple.

00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:55.780
So one of the points I've made is that,

00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:57.860
let's try to modernize that framework

00:23:57.860 --> 00:24:00.140
to say that there's a
consistent level of regulation,

00:24:00.140 --> 00:24:01.560
especially if you're a smaller company.

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:03.030
You don't have the lawyers and accountants

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:05.250
and compliance officers to jump through

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:07.450
all of these four different
layers for review,

00:24:07.450 --> 00:24:09.200
and to the extent the capital is scarce

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:10.830
and work crews don't have to be hired

00:24:10.830 --> 00:24:12.280
and networks don't have to be built,

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:13.880
I don't want that innovative spirit

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:15.980
to be directed to other parts of the world

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:17.770
where you can deploy at scale.

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:18.620
And that's part of the reason

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:21.050
why we've been trying to work
with localities, like LA,

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:23.190
to update that framework,
to make it more streamlined,

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:25.500
and just easier and cheaper
to build these networks.

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:27.017
- So in effect, while China can just say,

00:24:27.017 --> 00:24:29.497
"All right, we're doing
this and no local province

00:24:29.497 --> 00:24:33.320
"can do anything about it," and
while that may work for now,

00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:35.010
it may make the process faster for now

00:24:35.010 --> 00:24:37.480
while you have to deal with
local states and all that,

00:24:37.480 --> 00:24:40.750
in the longterm, it probably
hurts their ability to innovate

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:42.920
because it's all coming from the top.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:45.580
You guys have more of a
slog to fight through now,

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:48.600
but the hope is that you're
creating the groundwork sort of

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:51.050
to allow as much competition as possible

00:24:51.050 --> 00:24:53.130
through the cell companies basically.

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:54.960
- Exactly right, and part of the reason

00:24:54.960 --> 00:24:56.900
why I want this regulatory modernization

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:58.700
is not just to compete
with other countries,

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:00.430
but also because we recognize,

00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:01.350
and this is one of the truths

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:03.770
that hardly ever gets
recognized in Washington,

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:05.540
that heavy-handed regulation

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:07.520
and multiple levels of regulation

00:25:07.520 --> 00:25:10.170
disproportionately
affect smaller companies.

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:13.090
It's the larger companies that
have the compliance resources

00:25:14.746 --> 00:25:15.890
that can jump through
a lot of these hoops.

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:17.020
Sure, it'll cost them money,

00:25:17.020 --> 00:25:19.280
but they actually have
the resources to comply.

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:21.530
We don't wanna squeeze out some
of the smaller competitors,

00:25:21.530 --> 00:25:23.400
and especially in the technology space.

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:24.980
We're trying to encourage
a lot more companies

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:25.930
to enter this space.

00:25:25.930 --> 00:25:28.030
We haven't talked about space itself yet.

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:30.140
Satellite companies and
fixed wireless companies

00:25:30.140 --> 00:25:31.680
and some of these newer companies,

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:33.580
they may not be able to get off the ground

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:36.300
if that regulatory framework is so onerous

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:39.160
that they can't raise capital,
they can't hire the lawyers,

00:25:39.160 --> 00:25:41.020
and they can never deploy a product.

00:25:41.020 --> 00:25:42.870
- Yeah, all right, so I'm
obviously gonna wanna spend

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:44.817
a lot more time talking about
the pure tech side of this

00:25:44.817 --> 00:25:47.570
and the Silicon Valley side,
which is not as you said,

00:25:47.570 --> 00:25:49.000
everyone thinks it's sort of your job,

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:50.110
but it's not quite your job.

00:25:50.110 --> 00:25:52.040
But, wait, let's just do the
space thing for a minute,

00:25:52.040 --> 00:25:52.930
'cause I think it's real interesting,

00:25:52.930 --> 00:25:56.290
because it quite literally
is the next frontier,

00:25:56.290 --> 00:25:58.090
if not the final frontier.

00:25:58.090 --> 00:26:01.000
How in the world do you
sit down with people,

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:04.360
and you have government people now trying

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:07.620
to deal with the ideas that Elon Musk

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:08.820
and all of these people
are coming in with.

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:11.510
We're privatizing a
lot of the space stuff.

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:14.040
How do you even come to a basic place

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:17.140
where you can say, well,
you can kinda do this,

00:26:17.140 --> 00:26:19.470
and I want you to be free,
but we can't totally do that.

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:21.520
I mean, how did that conversation even go?

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:22.353
- It's fascinating.

00:26:22.353 --> 00:26:24.080
First of all, it's just
intellectually fascinating to me.

00:26:24.080 --> 00:26:27.180
I've always loved space, and
the innovation that's happening

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:29.950
in space right now is just unprecedented.

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:33.110
The launches they're able
to do using reusable rockets

00:26:33.110 --> 00:26:34.890
have really decreased the cost element

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:36.955
of launching these things into space.

00:26:36.955 --> 00:26:38.750
- I mean, Elon literally
just sent a Tesla into orbit,

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:40.189
just 'cause he could do it.

00:26:40.189 --> 00:26:41.022
(laughing)

00:26:41.022 --> 00:26:42.002
Do you know what I mean?
- It's incredible.

00:26:42.002 --> 00:26:43.300
- You didn't have a
regulation around that,

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:45.120
that you could just send a car into space?

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:47.412
You didn't wanna....
- No, no, no.

00:26:47.412 --> 00:26:48.245
(laughing)

00:26:48.245 --> 00:26:49.660
Yeah, no, it didn't
fall within our purview,

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:52.130
but certain other things
do fall within our purview.

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:53.960
So over the last two and a half years,

00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:55.340
we were the first administration

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:56.500
to approve what are called

00:26:56.500 --> 00:27:00.060
non-geostationary satellite
orbit constellations, NGSOs.

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:01.710
But essentially, a lot of satellites

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:02.800
that are being launched by companies,

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:05.690
like SpaceX and OneWeb,
and probably Kuiper,

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:08.680
Amazon in the future,
hundreds if not thousands

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:10.470
of these satellites in low earth orbits,

00:27:10.470 --> 00:27:13.010
not deep into space, but
lower in the earth's orbits

00:27:13.010 --> 00:27:15.230
so that they can beam
internet access services

00:27:15.230 --> 00:27:17.340
at a speed and a price point
that would be comparable

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:19.890
to what you would get from
a terrestrial provider.

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:22.010
My position here is very
reflective of those principles

00:27:22.010 --> 00:27:23.460
we talked about from the beginning.

00:27:23.460 --> 00:27:24.870
Instead of preemptively regulating,

00:27:24.870 --> 00:27:26.390
deciding, okay, you can launch,

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:29.210
but all of you, no, we're
not going to let you do it,

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:31.180
trying to pick a business
case or a business plan

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:34.370
that's going to work and letting
them take the first crack,

00:27:34.370 --> 00:27:35.920
we're taking a more market-based approach.

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:39.040
Let's allow all of them to
launch in various stages

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:42.500
using certain parameters for
orbital safety and whatnot,

00:27:42.500 --> 00:27:43.470
and see what happens.

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:46.020
Let's let the market, the
aggregated decisions of consumers,

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:47.990
decide which one will succeed.

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:49.380
Some of them might, you never know.

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:51.390
It could be a residential
broadband product.

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:52.390
Some of them are very different.

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:54.290
They wanna do industrial
internet of Things.

00:27:54.290 --> 00:27:56.140
I met recently with a company
that just wants to deploy

00:27:56.140 --> 00:27:57.890
150 satellites around the globe

00:27:57.890 --> 00:27:59.390
and they wanna sign up customers

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:00.840
like say the Department of Defense

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:03.910
that has military all around the world,

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:06.190
and they just need essentially
low bandwidth services

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:08.440
just to let people know
where the troops are

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:09.870
at any given point in time.

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:12.370
We want all of them to be
able to succeed in space.

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:13.203
- It's funny, you don't really think

00:28:13.203 --> 00:28:15.960
about the orbital safety portion of this,

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:17.110
as you just mentioned,

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:18.940
so it's almost like no
matter what happens,

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:21.590
you guys almost always need
another office to open, right?

00:28:21.590 --> 00:28:22.750
I mean, is that sorta how it works?

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:24.406
- Yeah.
- 'Cause you're like,

00:28:24.406 --> 00:28:25.887
"All right, I want them to
put the satellites up there,

00:28:25.887 --> 00:28:27.977
"we want to get people
internet as fast as possible

00:28:27.977 --> 00:28:29.510
"and as cheap as possible, it's all good."

00:28:29.510 --> 00:28:30.877
And then someone probably
walks in and is like,

00:28:30.877 --> 00:28:32.937
"You know, we gotta make
sure these satellites

00:28:32.937 --> 00:28:34.160
"aren't crashing into each other."

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:35.290
And then, what do you do?

00:28:35.290 --> 00:28:37.090
You do another study?

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:38.420
What are you mapping out there even?

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:39.253
- That's a good question.

00:28:39.253 --> 00:28:40.980
So that goes to the
earlier question you asked,

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:42.100
what if there were no regulations?

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:43.900
So here is where there is a base level

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:45.250
of regulation that's really important,

00:28:45.250 --> 00:28:46.580
especially in the space environment.

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:48.600
For those of you who saw "Gravity"

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:49.760
with Sandra Bullock and George Clooney.

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:50.730
- Right, we can't have these things

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:52.070
just spinning around rocking each other.

00:28:52.070 --> 00:28:53.140
- Exactly, because that stuff lasts

00:28:53.140 --> 00:28:54.730
for thousands and thousands of years,

00:28:54.730 --> 00:28:55.690
especially if they don't descend

00:28:55.690 --> 00:28:56.890
in the atmosphere and burn up.

00:28:56.890 --> 00:28:58.820
And so we wanna make sure
that that space environment

00:28:58.820 --> 00:28:59.760
is safe for everybody.

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:02.280
And so one of the things I
kicked off about a year ago,

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:04.830
let's see how should we update
our orbital debris rules?

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:06.460
They were first adopted in 2004,

00:29:06.460 --> 00:29:08.140
but back then we didn't
have a lot of satellites

00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:08.973
up in space.

00:29:08.973 --> 00:29:11.030
We're talking about a
lot more stuff in space,

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:13.350
and so accordingly I
think our old debris rules

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:14.183
need to be updated.

00:29:14.183 --> 00:29:15.870
So we're in the middle of conversation now

00:29:15.870 --> 00:29:18.050
about how should we think
about updating those rules,

00:29:18.050 --> 00:29:20.150
requiring companies to be safe.

00:29:20.150 --> 00:29:22.940
Should they have to deorbit
on a certain schedule,

00:29:22.940 --> 00:29:25.140
use certain technology,
that kind of thing.

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:27.070
- So I assume you'd prefer that everything

00:29:27.070 --> 00:29:29.750
has some sort of time limit
up there or something,

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:32.010
so that eventually it doesn't just become

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:33.150
space garbage somewhere?

00:29:33.150 --> 00:29:34.087
- Yeah, that's one of the things--

00:29:34.087 --> 00:29:35.980
- Now we're in "Futurama" or something.

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:37.050
- Yeah, exactly, that's one of the things

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:39.000
we're thinking about is
should there be a time limit,

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:40.460
should there be a built-in technology

00:29:40.460 --> 00:29:42.560
so some of these satellites
can deorbit automatically

00:29:42.560 --> 00:29:43.940
after a certain period of time,

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:47.100
or if there is a failure, they
will automatically deorbit,

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:49.410
things like that, and so
ultimately we wanna make sure

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:51.980
that everybody can succeed
in space in the long run.

00:29:51.980 --> 00:29:54.130
We don't want to, like I said,

00:29:54.130 --> 00:29:56.510
any of these orbital
slots, if you have debris,

00:29:56.510 --> 00:29:57.820
that's a generational problem,

00:29:57.820 --> 00:29:59.580
not just a multi-year problem.

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:02.570
- How much of this is
sort of longterm thinking,

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:04.060
say the way you would talk

00:30:04.060 --> 00:30:05.720
about the climate change situation?

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:09.870
So it's sort of like you
can plot a certain degree,

00:30:09.870 --> 00:30:11.480
but no matter how much you plot,

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:13.780
other countries, I mean
you just referenced China,

00:30:13.780 --> 00:30:15.360
other countries are just gonna do things

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:17.210
the way they want and how complicated

00:30:17.210 --> 00:30:19.200
that makes your job.
- It's incredible.

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:22.230
- Meaning you could do
everything exactly perfectly,

00:30:22.230 --> 00:30:24.560
and still if some other country decides

00:30:24.560 --> 00:30:25.393
to do whatever they want,

00:30:25.393 --> 00:30:27.830
you still have all of those problems.

00:30:27.830 --> 00:30:29.210
- That is a problem, so there is a lot

00:30:29.210 --> 00:30:31.280
of international coordination
that has to happen,

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:32.700
especially when it
comes to orbital debris.

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:35.000
We're always working with
some of the other countries,

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:37.070
agencies, to make sure
we're all on the same page,

00:30:37.070 --> 00:30:38.790
and there are international organizations

00:30:38.790 --> 00:30:40.740
that deal specifically with this.

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:42.660
In fact, shortly coming up in Egypt,

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:44.360
there's a big quadrennial,

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:46.470
every four years they have this conference

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:48.163
to talk about spectrum issues,

00:30:49.062 --> 00:30:50.640
and one of the issues is what spectrum

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:52.920
should be available for
satellite companies.

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:54.470
So in that context, we're always talking

00:30:54.470 --> 00:30:56.070
about being on the same page on things

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:58.670
like orbital debris and
spectrum allocation.

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:00.300
But I think you raised
a really good point,

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:01.870
is that we could do everything correctly,

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:04.670
but other countries are
looking to take the lead

00:31:04.670 --> 00:31:06.730
on things like artificial
intelligence and machine learning

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:08.530
or blockchain or quantum computing,

00:31:08.530 --> 00:31:11.030
and we don't have
jurisdiction over all of it,

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:13.530
but to the extent we
can, we wanna make sure

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:15.750
we advance U.S. leadership
because I do think

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:17.970
there are some first mover advantages

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:20.768
to countries and to
sectors that really show

00:31:20.768 --> 00:31:22.930
that they're willing to take the lead.

00:31:22.930 --> 00:31:24.420
- All right, let's take this back

00:31:24.420 --> 00:31:25.887
to this planet for a moment.

00:31:25.887 --> 00:31:26.720
(laughing)

00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:28.760
All right, let's really
dive into the tech stuff,

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:30.330
because it's obviously
near and dear to me.

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:31.440
Now first off, can you explain

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:34.950
how this is not fully your jurisdiction?

00:31:34.950 --> 00:31:36.840
Because this is one of
the interesting things,

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:38.650
where I mentioned right before we started,

00:31:38.650 --> 00:31:41.010
you've got Elizabeth Warren on the left

00:31:41.010 --> 00:31:42.550
calling for big tech regulation,

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:43.920
you've got Tucker Carlson on the right

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:46.220
calling for big tech regulation,

00:31:46.220 --> 00:31:48.610
and it's like, all
right, that's a strange,

00:31:48.610 --> 00:31:51.347
that's that little horseshoe
thing that everyone's going,

00:31:51.347 --> 00:31:52.667
"Well, how did this end up happening

00:31:52.667 --> 00:31:53.810
"that these two people want it?"

00:31:53.810 --> 00:31:56.590
They want it for very different reasons.

00:31:56.590 --> 00:31:57.830
But can you first explain

00:31:57.830 --> 00:31:59.910
how this isn't exactly your jurisdiction?

00:31:59.910 --> 00:32:01.730
'Cause I think everyone
sorta thinks it is.

00:32:01.730 --> 00:32:03.820
When they say government involvement,

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:05.410
I think they mean the FCC.

00:32:05.410 --> 00:32:07.130
- Yeah, they often do think the FCC,

00:32:07.130 --> 00:32:09.060
and especially in the
context of some of the more

00:32:09.060 --> 00:32:11.670
political salient issues, like
net neutrality they would.

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:13.500
So in a nutshell, under current law

00:32:13.500 --> 00:32:15.790
as Congress has set it
forth for many, many years,

00:32:15.790 --> 00:32:18.370
the FCC has jurisdiction
over the networks themselves,

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:19.750
the guts of the networks.

00:32:19.750 --> 00:32:21.630
We don't necessarily have jurisdiction,

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:24.890
or we don't have jurisdiction
over the content companies

00:32:24.890 --> 00:32:26.947
that ride over those networks,
and so we don't directly--

00:32:26.947 --> 00:32:29.270
- So the infrastructure
basically, the pipes.

00:32:29.270 --> 00:32:31.040
- Yes, exactly, so the infrastructure,

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:33.350
not so much the content
that goes over those pipes.

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:35.240
And so that's the fundamental distinction.

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:37.300
To the extent that an agency
does have jurisdiction,

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:38.790
primarily the Federal Trade Commission

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:40.790
and the Department of
Justice, each of which

00:32:40.790 --> 00:32:42.670
is now taking a look at some
of those tech companies.

00:32:42.670 --> 00:32:46.390
- Yeah, what is your, I mean,
I don't know if you can speak

00:32:46.390 --> 00:32:49.370
to this as the chairman of the FCC,

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:51.530
but what do you sort
of see as the right way

00:32:51.530 --> 00:32:54.160
to deal with the censorship issues,

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:56.963
the shadow banning,
algorithm manipulations,

00:32:58.240 --> 00:33:00.300
suppressing search results,
all of these things?

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:03.140
Do you have a general philosophy
on how to deal with this?

00:33:03.140 --> 00:33:06.640
- I think that obviously
these companies have thrived

00:33:06.640 --> 00:33:08.600
with a more light touch approach,

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:10.840
but a couple of years ago, I did flag,

00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:12.555
before it was cool, I
will note by the way,

00:33:12.555 --> 00:33:13.388
(Dave laughing)

00:33:13.388 --> 00:33:15.080
I did flag the fact that consumers

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:17.670
legitimately had some
concerns, as did lawmakers,

00:33:17.670 --> 00:33:19.530
about the lack of transparency

00:33:19.530 --> 00:33:21.250
on how some of these companies operated.

00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:23.810
We don't have insight into how Facebook

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:25.720
orders articles in its newsfeed.

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:27.930
We didn't have insight into how Twitter

00:33:27.930 --> 00:33:31.290
might decide to organize Twitter moments,

00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:32.890
or with Google and YouTube,

00:33:32.890 --> 00:33:35.290
we didn't have insight into demonetization

00:33:35.290 --> 00:33:36.517
and things like that, and I said,

00:33:36.517 --> 00:33:38.977
"Look, we need to have a
consistent level of regulation

00:33:38.977 --> 00:33:40.617
"across the entire internet economy.

00:33:40.617 --> 00:33:42.587
"It shouldn't matter whether
you're a network operator

00:33:42.587 --> 00:33:44.167
"or a content provider, for example,

00:33:44.167 --> 00:33:45.990
"when it comes to privacy."

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:47.770
As an online consumer, I don't care

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:49.640
whether it's a network
company or a content provider

00:33:49.640 --> 00:33:51.630
that has my sensitive information.

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:53.860
I want that information to be protected.

00:33:53.860 --> 00:33:55.800
I called a couple of years
ago for a conversation

00:33:55.800 --> 00:33:57.740
about how should we think
about the tech sector,

00:33:57.740 --> 00:33:58.660
some of these tech giants

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:00.460
that have been completely unregulated.

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:02.740
And now it's interesting
to see, as you mentioned,

00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:04.690
people from across the political spectrum

00:34:04.690 --> 00:34:06.690
are reaching this sort of odd consensus

00:34:06.690 --> 00:34:07.820
that something needs to be done.

00:34:07.820 --> 00:34:09.910
What that something
is, I don't quite know,

00:34:09.910 --> 00:34:11.260
and it's up to Congress to say,

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:13.050
but it is interesting how the conversation

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:14.350
has evolved here the last couple of years.

00:34:14.350 --> 00:34:15.950
- Is this becoming one of the weird ones

00:34:15.950 --> 00:34:17.040
that you sorta referenced before

00:34:17.040 --> 00:34:19.850
where in a weird way,
the big boys in the game,

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:21.820
they kinda do want regulation now

00:34:21.820 --> 00:34:24.640
because they know that
they'll be able to survive.

00:34:24.640 --> 00:34:25.860
Google's got a lotta lawyers,

00:34:25.860 --> 00:34:27.610
but that the new guys coming up,

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:28.880
once there's more regulation,

00:34:28.880 --> 00:34:30.750
they're gonna have to
hit certain benchmarks

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:33.030
to do certain things, and they
just won't be able to do it.

00:34:33.030 --> 00:34:36.520
And also possibly that the
problems are now so huge,

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:38.190
especially around free speech,

00:34:38.190 --> 00:34:40.440
that it's like please regulate us.

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:42.330
That way we don't have to
think about the problems.

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:45.210
- It is very curious
how things have changed.

00:34:45.210 --> 00:34:47.250
So some of those companies
were strongly advocating

00:34:47.250 --> 00:34:50.520
for net neutrality regulations
coming from Washington,

00:34:50.520 --> 00:34:51.820
heavy-handed regulations applied

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:53.880
to some of their network operator rivals.

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:56.860
When California in particular
passed a law on this subject,

00:34:56.860 --> 00:34:58.710
those companies were very much behind it.

00:34:58.710 --> 00:35:00.280
But now the table somewhat turns.

00:35:00.280 --> 00:35:02.260
California's passed this
stringent privacy law,

00:35:02.260 --> 00:35:03.860
those very same companies
are coming to Washington

00:35:03.860 --> 00:35:05.827
and telling Congress, "We
need a federal privacy law

00:35:05.827 --> 00:35:08.280
"in order to get rid of this
patchwork of state regulation."

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:10.630
And I always say, under the
well-established precedent

00:35:10.630 --> 00:35:12.629
of goose versus gander, you
can't have it both ways.

00:35:12.629 --> 00:35:13.462
(Dave laughing)

00:35:13.462 --> 00:35:15.030
You have to be able to be
intellectually consistent

00:35:15.030 --> 00:35:17.020
on this issue, and that's
part of the reason why

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:19.380
on net neutrality, on privacy,
on any of these issues,

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:21.710
let's just have an open
intellectually honest conversation

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:24.370
about how to establish a
consistent level of regulation

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:26.090
that protects consumers and competition

00:35:26.090 --> 00:35:27.820
across the entire internet economy.

00:35:27.820 --> 00:35:28.720
- I have to write that one down.

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:30.390
I'm not familiar with goose versus gander.

00:35:30.390 --> 00:35:31.327
(laughing)

00:35:31.327 --> 00:35:33.540
I'll make a note of that one
to check that one out later.

00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:35.553
- I don't have a copyright
on it yet, but stay tuned.

00:35:35.553 --> 00:35:37.030
(laughing)

00:35:37.030 --> 00:35:38.230
- Okay, so let's back up,

00:35:38.230 --> 00:35:40.340
'cause you mentioned net
neutrality a couple of times.

00:35:40.340 --> 00:35:42.440
Now about a year and a half ago or so,

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:43.830
when net neutrality disappeared,

00:35:43.830 --> 00:35:44.770
if you were paying attention

00:35:44.770 --> 00:35:48.450
to the sorta mainstream
take on it, this was it.

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:50.430
World War III was starting,
the internet was over,

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:51.610
nobody was gonna get video,

00:35:51.610 --> 00:35:53.560
everybody was gonna be throttled,

00:35:53.560 --> 00:35:55.293
corporations were gonna take over.

00:35:57.010 --> 00:35:57.900
Here we are.

00:35:57.900 --> 00:35:59.320
- It's amazing.

00:35:59.320 --> 00:36:01.540
- All right, can you
absolutely just for the people

00:36:01.540 --> 00:36:04.370
that really want just like the
cleanest clearest explanation

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:06.680
so we can clip this perfectly,

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:10.410
what was net neutrality
and what actually happened?

00:36:10.410 --> 00:36:13.010
- So net neutrality involves
the basic question of how,

00:36:13.010 --> 00:36:16.350
if at all, should the FCC
treat internet access.

00:36:16.350 --> 00:36:18.660
Should it regulate it under
what is called Title II,

00:36:18.660 --> 00:36:21.460
which was the 1930s
regulations that were developed

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:24.050
to regulate Ma Ball, the
old telephone monopoly,

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:26.240
or should it regulate the
internet under Title I,

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:27.770
the more market-based approach

00:36:27.770 --> 00:36:30.640
that governed the internet
from 1996 until 2015?

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:34.290
My view is the market-based
approach served us very well.

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:37.930
We got $1.5 trillion of
network investment companies,

00:36:37.930 --> 00:36:39.700
like some of those that we've mentioned

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:42.160
becoming giants from startups,

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:44.960
and consumers benefiting
from all that innovation.

00:36:44.960 --> 00:36:47.070
At the end of the day,
I look at the evidence

00:36:47.070 --> 00:36:47.903
that's in the record.

00:36:47.903 --> 00:36:50.730
When we made our decision of 2017,

00:36:50.730 --> 00:36:52.450
I kept track of some of those predictions.

00:36:52.450 --> 00:36:54.500
You're going to have to pay $5 per tweet.

00:36:54.500 --> 00:36:57.610
You're not going to be
allowed to post on Instagram.

00:36:57.610 --> 00:36:59.570
One of my favorites, the
Senate Democratic Caucus

00:36:59.570 --> 00:37:02.160
Twitter account said
the internet will work

00:37:02.160 --> 00:37:03.890
one word at a time.

00:37:03.890 --> 00:37:06.650
Or one of my favorites, this
is the end of the internet

00:37:06.650 --> 00:37:07.483
as we know it.

00:37:07.483 --> 00:37:09.150
Well, fast forward to now.

00:37:09.150 --> 00:37:10.510
Internet speeds in the United States

00:37:10.510 --> 00:37:13.130
on average are up over 50%,

00:37:13.130 --> 00:37:16.030
network investments in
2018 was up $3 billion,

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:17.620
the second in consecutive increase,

00:37:17.620 --> 00:37:19.420
more fiber was deployed in 2018

00:37:19.420 --> 00:37:21.790
than in any year since
they've been keeping records,

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:25.347
and venture capital, funding
startups, set a record in 2018.

00:37:25.347 --> 00:37:27.670
And one wouldn't expect
that if these internet

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:29.970
service providers were in
fact acting as a gatekeeper,

00:37:29.970 --> 00:37:31.650
chocking off innovation.

00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:32.710
So to those critics out there,

00:37:32.710 --> 00:37:34.350
I would say the record is clear.

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:36.680
The internet is more open than ever,

00:37:36.680 --> 00:37:39.080
and thanks to our decision,
more people than ever before,

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:40.510
faster than ever before,

00:37:40.510 --> 00:37:42.560
can hate tweet their
favorite FCC chairman.

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:44.157
So I think it's a
testament to our success.

00:37:44.157 --> 00:37:47.207
- Right, so you got an
unbelievable amount of hate

00:37:47.207 --> 00:37:50.200
during that time.
- Oh my god, yeah, no kidding.

00:37:50.200 --> 00:37:51.730
- That might have led
you to wanna regulate

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:53.710
some of this a little bit more.

00:37:53.710 --> 00:37:55.740
But I guess you stuck with your ideal.

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:57.840
- Yeah, no, we did, and
there was a lot of pressure

00:37:57.840 --> 00:37:59.300
on the other side, no question about it,

00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:01.800
and personal threats made
against me and my family

00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:03.010
and even the agency itself.

00:38:03.010 --> 00:38:04.360
There are people in jail today

00:38:04.360 --> 00:38:07.240
for threatening my family and the agency.

00:38:07.240 --> 00:38:11.580
But at the end of the day,
I really have the conviction

00:38:11.580 --> 00:38:14.110
that we were doing the right thing.

00:38:14.110 --> 00:38:16.330
I've got a quote by my computer

00:38:16.330 --> 00:38:17.967
and I see it every single
day when I power up,

00:38:17.967 --> 00:38:20.150
and it's from Churchill back in the '20s.

00:38:20.150 --> 00:38:22.250
It said, hear this, young men and women,

00:38:22.250 --> 00:38:23.810
and proclaim it far and wide,

00:38:23.810 --> 00:38:26.080
the earth is yours and
the fullness thereof.

00:38:26.080 --> 00:38:27.610
Be kind, but be fierce.

00:38:27.610 --> 00:38:29.810
You are needed now more than ever before.

00:38:29.810 --> 00:38:32.590
Take up the mantle of
change, for now is your time.

00:38:32.590 --> 00:38:34.090
And I know it sounds cheesy,

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:35.740
but I really do try to embrace that ethos

00:38:35.740 --> 00:38:37.950
that you've gotta know what you believe

00:38:37.950 --> 00:38:39.980
and you've got to execute on that belief

00:38:39.980 --> 00:38:41.040
to the best of your ability

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:42.790
and take the arrows that come with it.

00:38:42.790 --> 00:38:44.290
Is it going to be popular always?

00:38:44.290 --> 00:38:45.123
No, not at all.

00:38:45.123 --> 00:38:46.390
I mean, I understand a lot of people

00:38:46.390 --> 00:38:48.490
still are angry about this decision.

00:38:48.490 --> 00:38:51.110
But at the end of the day,
I've got the longterm in mind,

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:53.640
and the longterm is one in
which the digital divide closes,

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:55.170
everyone's connected with the internet,

00:38:55.170 --> 00:38:56.410
the internet is free and open,

00:38:56.410 --> 00:38:59.350
and that's the vision that
we're gonna continue to execute.

00:38:59.350 --> 00:39:01.690
- Do you think just to
sorta give the people

00:39:01.690 --> 00:39:03.610
that want it to keep
net neutrality in place,

00:39:03.610 --> 00:39:06.640
to give the devil his due let's say,

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:10.330
do you think that it's just a
fundamental misunderstanding

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:12.393
of what companies do?

00:39:12.393 --> 00:39:15.450
The fears seem to be that
if you don't regulate them

00:39:15.450 --> 00:39:20.450
in this way that one company
will slow down Netflix

00:39:20.590 --> 00:39:23.430
and the other one will speed
up their own broadband service

00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:24.610
and all of these things.

00:39:24.610 --> 00:39:27.530
And there is some like initial thought

00:39:27.530 --> 00:39:28.810
that kinda makes sense there,

00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:30.470
like you would wanna hurt your competitors

00:39:30.470 --> 00:39:31.700
if you're in several businesses,

00:39:31.700 --> 00:39:33.720
and you'd wanna help your partners,

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:36.690
but that's not really
how competition works.

00:39:36.690 --> 00:39:38.170
And I guess everything
that you just laid out

00:39:38.170 --> 00:39:40.274
is sorta proof of that.

00:39:40.274 --> 00:39:41.470
Do you think that they
just sort of fundamentally

00:39:41.470 --> 00:39:46.423
misunderstand economics
or something like that?

00:39:47.650 --> 00:39:49.530
- I understand the concern they
might have in the abstract,

00:39:49.530 --> 00:39:51.780
but the two things I
always try to emphasize

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:54.180
to allay those concerns are, number one,

00:39:54.180 --> 00:39:58.840
I support Congress putting
on the page in the modern era

00:39:58.840 --> 00:40:00.960
what the rules of the road should be.

00:40:00.960 --> 00:40:02.500
It's a one-page bill, honestly,

00:40:02.500 --> 00:40:04.070
that Congress could pass tomorrow.

00:40:04.070 --> 00:40:06.100
No blocking of lawful
content on the internet,

00:40:06.100 --> 00:40:07.910
no throttling of lawful content,

00:40:07.910 --> 00:40:10.310
no anti-competitive paid prioritization.

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:12.780
Transparency, make sure every
internet service provider

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:16.240
documents all its business and
network management practices

00:40:16.240 --> 00:40:17.210
for everyone to see.

00:40:17.210 --> 00:40:20.660
I've just outlined a 99% supportable bill,

00:40:20.660 --> 00:40:21.950
but that's never going to happen because--

00:40:21.950 --> 00:40:23.734
- There's no such thing
as a one-page paper.

00:40:23.734 --> 00:40:24.620
(laughing)

00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:25.650
Come on, you gotta add in what,

00:40:25.650 --> 00:40:28.060
like 5,000 more pages.
- Yeah, all the wherefores.

00:40:28.060 --> 00:40:30.780
And 27 bills that have to do with farming.

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:32.700
- Exactly, but this is a very simple bill

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:33.550
that I would support.

00:40:33.550 --> 00:40:35.230
I think most members of
Congress would support.

00:40:35.230 --> 00:40:37.350
But the political impulses from those

00:40:37.350 --> 00:40:40.490
who really rabidly want
heavy-handed regulation,

00:40:40.490 --> 00:40:42.510
they're never going to
allow that to happen.

00:40:42.510 --> 00:40:43.700
The second thing I emphasize is,

00:40:43.700 --> 00:40:47.720
look, antitrust and
competition law has long been

00:40:47.720 --> 00:40:51.100
a very steadfast protector
of competition in this area,

00:40:51.100 --> 00:40:53.980
the FTC and the Department
of Justice in particular.

00:40:53.980 --> 00:40:56.710
The internet didn't die from 1996 to 2015

00:40:56.710 --> 00:40:58.290
when we had this exact same framework.

00:40:58.290 --> 00:41:00.910
To the contrary, it thrived
with a market-based approach.

00:41:00.910 --> 00:41:03.150
And just think about what
the internet would have been

00:41:03.150 --> 00:41:05.230
if we had treated it like Ma Bell.

00:41:05.230 --> 00:41:06.380
You would have an internet

00:41:06.380 --> 00:41:08.810
that was just as reliable as Amtrak,

00:41:08.810 --> 00:41:11.940
as popular as the post office,
as efficient as the DMV.

00:41:11.940 --> 00:41:14.500
That is the model that some
people want to return to,

00:41:14.500 --> 00:41:15.890
and to me, that's not good enough.

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:18.143
American consumers
deserve better than that.

00:41:19.120 --> 00:41:20.390
- I'm sure you've heard about this sort of

00:41:20.390 --> 00:41:22.420
platform verse publisher debate.

00:41:22.420 --> 00:41:23.713
That's about the stupidest
question I've ever asked,

00:41:23.713 --> 00:41:24.822
and I'm going to go there.

00:41:24.822 --> 00:41:25.655
- No, not at all.

00:41:25.655 --> 00:41:26.488
- I'm gonna go out on a limb

00:41:26.488 --> 00:41:28.450
and assume you've heard about this debate.

00:41:28.450 --> 00:41:29.740
So for me, as a guy that wants

00:41:29.740 --> 00:41:31.350
as little regulation as possible,

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:34.500
so I totally hear your philosophy on this,

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:36.130
the platform verse publisher one

00:41:36.130 --> 00:41:37.790
is starting to make sense to me.

00:41:37.790 --> 00:41:40.560
This is a place where I
could start seeing a need

00:41:40.560 --> 00:41:42.730
for regulation because if
you're just a platform,

00:41:42.730 --> 00:41:44.870
then you should have to do all the things

00:41:44.870 --> 00:41:47.590
that you want in your one-pager.

00:41:47.590 --> 00:41:48.660
But if you're a publisher,

00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:51.120
then you have those
extra responsibilities.

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:52.900
Yet in a weird way, the platforms,

00:41:52.900 --> 00:41:54.750
say Twitter, YouTube, Facebook,

00:41:54.750 --> 00:41:59.750
are behaving like publishers
and not platforms.

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:02.880
How complex is all of this to unpack?

00:42:02.880 --> 00:42:04.620
- It's exceptionally complex,

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:06.170
and one of the fascinating things to watch

00:42:06.170 --> 00:42:08.640
from my vantage point is on Capitol Hill,

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:11.020
you're now seeing
Republicans and Democrats

00:42:11.020 --> 00:42:13.760
openly wondering should
we change the laws?

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:15.850
Let's hold hearings to examine
how we should change the laws

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:18.063
to accommodate this new understanding.

00:42:19.751 --> 00:42:21.692
Everything seems to be
up for grabs right now,

00:42:21.692 --> 00:42:22.840
and I'm now sure how
it's going to play out,

00:42:22.840 --> 00:42:24.950
but that debate of
platform versus publisher

00:42:24.950 --> 00:42:27.380
is something that I think
was pretty much unthinkable

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:29.790
just five years ago, so I'm not
sure where it's going to go,

00:42:29.790 --> 00:42:31.120
but we're certainly watching it.

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:33.340
- Do you think it's possible
that there are certain things

00:42:33.340 --> 00:42:36.550
that the government is just
no longer equipped to handle?

00:42:36.550 --> 00:42:39.950
That the power and the
amount of information,

00:42:39.950 --> 00:42:41.680
the amount of technology

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:45.690
that Google let's say controls right now,

00:42:45.690 --> 00:42:48.540
is so awesome and unprecedented,

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:52.100
that the idea that a government
regulatory commission

00:42:52.100 --> 00:42:54.680
could actually harness
it, fully understand it,

00:42:54.680 --> 00:42:56.300
and fully guide it in the right way,

00:42:56.300 --> 00:42:58.400
that that almost is impossible?

00:42:58.400 --> 00:42:59.233
- That's a really good question.

00:42:59.233 --> 00:43:00.920
- That must be a pretty
crappy thing to think about,

00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:02.348
I suppose, given your position.

00:43:02.348 --> 00:43:03.551
(laughing)

00:43:03.551 --> 00:43:04.384
But I mean, do you think that is possible,

00:43:04.384 --> 00:43:06.620
that these companies have grown in ways,

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:09.660
like the amount of power that Google has

00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:12.480
is way more than Ma Bell had in 1930,

00:43:12.480 --> 00:43:16.010
and government obviously
changes much slower

00:43:16.010 --> 00:43:18.070
than a technology company can.

00:43:18.070 --> 00:43:19.320
- That's a good question.

00:43:20.860 --> 00:43:21.693
I'd have to think about
it a little deeper.

00:43:21.693 --> 00:43:23.700
I would say the first
cut, I do think the tools

00:43:23.700 --> 00:43:26.840
of antitrust and competition
law are fairly well-suited

00:43:26.840 --> 00:43:29.940
to deal with companies
that may have market power.

00:43:29.940 --> 00:43:33.080
The Standard Oils of yesteryear
could just have easily,

00:43:33.080 --> 00:43:34.780
that framework could just as easily apply

00:43:34.780 --> 00:43:37.520
to the tech giants of today
and to evaluate whether or not

00:43:37.520 --> 00:43:39.870
they are posing an
anti-competitive threat.

00:43:39.870 --> 00:43:42.030
What I would say is the central problem

00:43:42.030 --> 00:43:43.729
we haven't yet wrestled with

00:43:43.729 --> 00:43:45.790
is that the pace of
technological innovation

00:43:45.790 --> 00:43:47.260
is quickening, if anything,

00:43:47.260 --> 00:43:50.410
and we're now rapidly
dealing with not just laws

00:43:50.410 --> 00:43:52.430
that are out of date, but a Congress

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:54.840
that in a regulatory structure

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:56.710
that isn't equipped to deal nimbly

00:43:56.710 --> 00:43:57.570
with some of the challenges.

00:43:57.570 --> 00:44:00.440
To give you one example,
facial recognition technology.

00:44:00.440 --> 00:44:01.970
You're now seeing a lot
of people wondering,

00:44:01.970 --> 00:44:04.160
well, should we ban or should we regulate

00:44:04.160 --> 00:44:05.560
facial recognition technology?

00:44:05.560 --> 00:44:06.480
How does it work?

00:44:06.480 --> 00:44:09.020
Or cryptocurrencies, you
see regulators struggling

00:44:09.020 --> 00:44:10.230
with how should we think about this?

00:44:10.230 --> 00:44:13.180
Is it another financial instrument

00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:15.210
that we should regulate
under the old laws,

00:44:15.210 --> 00:44:17.210
or should we just come
up with something new?

00:44:17.210 --> 00:44:19.340
Across all of these different sectors,

00:44:19.340 --> 00:44:21.520
the pace is so fast that by the time

00:44:21.520 --> 00:44:23.500
we even understand what it is,

00:44:23.500 --> 00:44:25.460
it's just almost too
late to pass regulations

00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:27.290
because the marketplace
has moved beyond it.

00:44:27.290 --> 00:44:29.010
So I think that is one
of the central problems

00:44:29.010 --> 00:44:30.650
that we haven't yet grappled with.

00:44:30.650 --> 00:44:32.960
- Is the other part just
that administrations

00:44:32.960 --> 00:44:34.610
change pretty quickly?

00:44:34.610 --> 00:44:36.440
We don't know who the president's gonna be

00:44:36.440 --> 00:44:38.580
in about a year and a half
from now, we just don't.

00:44:38.580 --> 00:44:42.330
And the next person could
come in, and is your term,

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:44.580
is that just on the
decision of the president

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:45.870
or does that relate to your term?

00:44:45.870 --> 00:44:48.570
- No, so every chairman is a commissioner,

00:44:48.570 --> 00:44:51.233
so I have a commissioner
term that ends in 2022,

00:44:52.110 --> 00:44:54.460
but by law the president has to designate

00:44:54.460 --> 00:44:56.480
whoever the chairman is at
any given point in time.

00:44:56.480 --> 00:44:59.720
So yeah, I do, I have a
term that runs independent

00:44:59.720 --> 00:45:01.490
of the presidential administration

00:45:01.490 --> 00:45:02.360
that happens to be in power.

00:45:02.360 --> 00:45:05.728
- But technically the next
president could just take you

00:45:05.728 --> 00:45:07.550
outta that term.
- Exactly.

00:45:07.550 --> 00:45:09.150
- So how much of your
consideration has to do

00:45:09.150 --> 00:45:12.740
with just the political
realities of all of this?

00:45:12.740 --> 00:45:13.573
- To me, zero.

00:45:13.573 --> 00:45:15.630
One of the great things about
being an independent agency

00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:19.300
is that we make the decision
that we think is the right one.

00:45:19.300 --> 00:45:22.150
Generally speaking, our
initiatives have been nonpartisan

00:45:22.150 --> 00:45:23.270
in nature, to me at least.

00:45:23.270 --> 00:45:25.730
Getting broadband to rural
Americans and tribal Americans

00:45:25.730 --> 00:45:27.323
is not a Republican issue
or Democratic issue,

00:45:27.323 --> 00:45:29.110
it's just an American issue.

00:45:29.110 --> 00:45:31.800
Getting people with disabilities
access to technology,

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:34.410
helping people in hurricane ravished areas

00:45:34.410 --> 00:45:36.450
is not a partisan issue as well.

00:45:36.450 --> 00:45:38.040
5G is a national imperative.

00:45:38.040 --> 00:45:40.410
So a lot of these things are
actually bipartisan in nature

00:45:40.410 --> 00:45:42.950
or nonpartisan I would
say, and so to me at least,

00:45:42.950 --> 00:45:44.640
that's how I tried to embrace the job.

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:46.170
And the other thing that goes unremarked

00:45:46.170 --> 00:45:48.390
in Washington at least,
is as far as we can tell

00:45:48.390 --> 00:45:50.560
I'm the first chairman in history ever

00:45:50.560 --> 00:45:52.150
to ask each one of the commissioners,

00:45:52.150 --> 00:45:54.650
Republican and Democrat,
to take leadership

00:45:54.650 --> 00:45:56.950
to speak for the agency
on an issue of importance.

00:45:56.950 --> 00:45:58.350
Traditionally, the chairman essentially

00:45:58.350 --> 00:46:01.500
hogs all of the responsibility
for himself or herself,

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:02.970
but I've tried to delegate a lot of that

00:46:02.970 --> 00:46:05.200
to make sure that all
of them feel empowered.

00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:07.060
That's good for them, but
it's also good for the agency

00:46:07.060 --> 00:46:09.420
to show that, look, we're
not a political agency

00:46:09.420 --> 00:46:12.400
that rises or falls based
on where the political winds

00:46:12.400 --> 00:46:13.350
may happen to take us.

00:46:13.350 --> 00:46:16.290
- You must be pretty enthused
that things like this exist

00:46:16.290 --> 00:46:17.530
for you to get your message out

00:46:17.530 --> 00:46:18.840
versus in the old days.
- Oh my god.

00:46:18.840 --> 00:46:20.980
- To the extent that
somebody might wanna hear

00:46:20.980 --> 00:46:23.900
some wonky stuff from the FCC chairman,

00:46:23.900 --> 00:46:25.850
it was basically done on a five-minute

00:46:25.850 --> 00:46:27.781
Sunday morning talk show.
- Oh yeah.

00:46:27.781 --> 00:46:28.614
- And it's like now you can actually

00:46:28.614 --> 00:46:29.660
really expand on these things.

00:46:29.660 --> 00:46:31.610
- That's why I'm so grateful
to outlets like this one.

00:46:31.610 --> 00:46:33.910
I'm telling you, as somebody,
I mean, a lot of your viewers

00:46:33.910 --> 00:46:34.930
are probably much younger than me,

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:37.050
but as somebody who
grew up in the late '70s

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:39.060
and early '80s in rural Kansas,

00:46:39.060 --> 00:46:41.540
there were very few
ways to get information

00:46:41.540 --> 00:46:43.190
from the outside world, and virtually none

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:45.140
to get information translated from you

00:46:45.140 --> 00:46:46.700
to get your voice heard.

00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:49.820
So to go from that where we
were 30, 40 years ago to now

00:46:49.820 --> 00:46:52.690
where essentially the internet
has democratized speech

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:55.530
in a way that is unthinkable,
is incredible to me.

00:46:55.530 --> 00:46:57.920
One example I always use is I
was the first FCC commissioner

00:46:57.920 --> 00:46:59.743
on Twitter back in 2012.

00:46:59.743 --> 00:47:01.502
When I joined, some of my
colleagues said, "This is crazy."

00:47:01.502 --> 00:47:03.027
- May God have mercy on your soul.

00:47:03.027 --> 00:47:03.940
- "You shouldn't be doing this."

00:47:03.940 --> 00:47:06.390
But now it's enabled me to
get the message out to people

00:47:06.390 --> 00:47:08.270
who are never gonna be
able to come to Washington

00:47:08.270 --> 00:47:10.870
or hire somebody to come to
Washington on their behalf.

00:47:10.870 --> 00:47:12.710
It's had a real impact on my work

00:47:12.710 --> 00:47:14.530
and my ability to get my voice out.

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:15.740
There are a few issues
that I've championed

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:18.270
that have been a result
of people tweeting at me

00:47:18.270 --> 00:47:19.497
and I learn more about the issue

00:47:19.497 --> 00:47:21.260
and we end up taking action that helps,

00:47:21.260 --> 00:47:23.280
in this one case in
particular, save lives.

00:47:23.280 --> 00:47:26.060
The 911 calling system,
we've improved thanks in part

00:47:26.060 --> 00:47:28.200
to a tweet I got back in December of 2013.

00:47:28.200 --> 00:47:29.363
- What was that?

00:47:29.363 --> 00:47:30.230
What was the message?

00:47:30.230 --> 00:47:34.000
- So December 1st of 2013,
a woman named Kari Hunt Dunn

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:35.240
was living in Marshall, Texas.

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:38.250
She and her three kids went
to visit her estranged husband

00:47:38.250 --> 00:47:39.970
in a hotel room in Texas.

00:47:39.970 --> 00:47:40.920
As soon as she got into the hotel room,

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:43.100
her husband started stabbing her,

00:47:43.100 --> 00:47:44.500
and her nine-year-old daughter raced

00:47:44.500 --> 00:47:47.730
to the hotel room's phone
and started dialing 911,

00:47:47.730 --> 00:47:50.270
as everybody knows, but
the call didn't go through.

00:47:50.270 --> 00:47:53.150
She dialed 911 once
again, and one more time,

00:47:53.150 --> 00:47:54.280
and one more time.

00:47:54.280 --> 00:47:56.110
She didn't realize she
had to dial nine first

00:47:56.110 --> 00:47:57.690
to reach an outside line.

00:47:57.690 --> 00:48:01.170
So the story made the
local press down there.

00:48:01.170 --> 00:48:03.950
Somebody tweeted the story at
me, and I started reading up

00:48:03.950 --> 00:48:06.660
and I thought, boy,
that raised my antenna.

00:48:06.660 --> 00:48:08.970
What's the situation of 911
calling around the country?

00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:11.480
So two weeks later, I was
on the phone with Hank Hunt

00:48:11.480 --> 00:48:13.000
who was Kari's father.

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:14.480
I talked to him about the situation.

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:16.320
A month later, I sent a letter to the CEOs

00:48:16.320 --> 00:48:18.330
of the top 10 hotel chains
in the United States,

00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:20.570
as well as the Hotel
and Lodging Association.

00:48:20.570 --> 00:48:23.170
A year after that, I stood
next to Hank in Marshall, Texas

00:48:23.170 --> 00:48:24.940
where we updated the American public

00:48:24.940 --> 00:48:26.490
on the progress we had made.

00:48:26.490 --> 00:48:29.590
And last year, I stood with
Hank in the Oval Office

00:48:29.590 --> 00:48:31.280
as the president signed legislation

00:48:31.280 --> 00:48:33.760
named Kari's Law, after his late daughter,

00:48:33.760 --> 00:48:36.820
to establish no access code required,

00:48:36.820 --> 00:48:38.780
legislation that would essentially

00:48:38.780 --> 00:48:40.630
allow direct access to 911.

00:48:40.630 --> 00:48:41.710
And so to me at least,

00:48:41.710 --> 00:48:44.210
I know Twitter has been a plus and a minus

00:48:44.210 --> 00:48:47.240
for many, many people, but
to me at least that story,

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:48.940
it really vindicates the power

00:48:48.940 --> 00:48:51.610
of an individual like Hank
to get his story out there

00:48:51.610 --> 00:48:53.330
and to effect meaningful social change.

00:48:53.330 --> 00:48:54.570
- Yeah, I mean, that's pretty incredible,

00:48:54.570 --> 00:48:56.020
and actually the way you just laid it out,

00:48:56.020 --> 00:48:57.670
even with the dates,
I mean, you guys moved

00:48:57.670 --> 00:48:58.503
pretty quick on this.

00:48:58.503 --> 00:49:00.570
That doesn't sound like a kinda
governmental thing, right,

00:49:00.570 --> 00:49:02.960
like within a year or so.

00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:04.830
- One thing I think people appreciate,

00:49:04.830 --> 00:49:07.090
whether they like or don't
like the policies we pursue,

00:49:07.090 --> 00:49:09.040
is I tend to be a pretty energetic person.

00:49:09.040 --> 00:49:12.139
I wanna get things done and
I have an impatience about me

00:49:12.139 --> 00:49:15.330
that forces me to wanna take
action as quick as possible.

00:49:15.330 --> 00:49:18.770
So on an issue like that, which
naturally it sounds right,

00:49:18.770 --> 00:49:20.740
'cause no kid would think
that they have to...

00:49:20.740 --> 00:49:22.350
Many adults wouldn't realize
you have to press nine

00:49:22.350 --> 00:49:24.480
to get the outside number.

00:49:24.480 --> 00:49:26.460
Did you get any pushback
from the industry,

00:49:26.460 --> 00:49:29.580
that was like, "We don't
want outside access"

00:49:29.580 --> 00:49:30.760
or something like that?

00:49:30.760 --> 00:49:33.120
I mean, what would be the
pushback on something like that?

00:49:33.120 --> 00:49:34.590
- There wasn't a lot of pushback.

00:49:34.590 --> 00:49:37.670
It was more how difficult
would it be to retrofit

00:49:37.670 --> 00:49:39.330
some of the old telephone systems,

00:49:39.330 --> 00:49:41.530
or is the government going to mandate

00:49:41.530 --> 00:49:43.680
specific technology to be used?

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:45.160
I think they were
generally very supportive,

00:49:45.160 --> 00:49:47.910
and what I found in talking
to some of the hotel owners

00:49:47.910 --> 00:49:50.640
and phone manufacturers is
as soon as they became aware

00:49:50.640 --> 00:49:53.130
of the issue, they were more
than willing to make a change.

00:49:53.130 --> 00:49:55.010
In many cases, it doesn't cost any money.

00:49:55.010 --> 00:49:56.980
It's essentially just flipping
a switch, so to speak,

00:49:56.980 --> 00:50:00.010
in terms of software and you
get rid of that access code.

00:50:00.010 --> 00:50:01.030
To me, it's really gratifying.

00:50:01.030 --> 00:50:02.570
Whenever I check into a hotel room now

00:50:02.570 --> 00:50:06.000
I always look at the phone
as soon as I put down my key,

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:08.023
and it's great to see,

00:50:09.468 --> 00:50:11.830
to reach emergency
services, just dial 911.

00:50:11.830 --> 00:50:13.680
Millions of Americans who
check into hotel rooms

00:50:13.680 --> 00:50:16.070
will never know that the FCC,
that Hank had a part in that,

00:50:16.070 --> 00:50:18.540
but it goes to that point
you were making earlier,

00:50:18.540 --> 00:50:20.760
that the longer term
value that we delivered,

00:50:20.760 --> 00:50:23.480
even if it doesn't get attributed to us,

00:50:23.480 --> 00:50:24.690
it really makes me feel good to know

00:50:24.690 --> 00:50:26.020
that we made a positive difference.

00:50:26.020 --> 00:50:27.880
So you've mentioned Twitter,

00:50:27.880 --> 00:50:31.220
the good and the bad Twitter,
a couple of times here.

00:50:31.220 --> 00:50:32.450
We should relate some of this

00:50:32.450 --> 00:50:33.950
to generally just the election,

00:50:33.950 --> 00:50:34.900
'cause I don't know if you know this,

00:50:34.900 --> 00:50:36.840
but we're in an election year now, right?

00:50:36.840 --> 00:50:37.673
- Is that right?
- There's something coming.

00:50:37.673 --> 00:50:39.060
- I had no idea.

00:50:39.060 --> 00:50:41.537
- And all of the sort
of the hate and the good

00:50:41.537 --> 00:50:43.650
and all of it, it's all kinda ramping up,

00:50:43.650 --> 00:50:45.500
the censorship stuff and all of that.

00:50:46.460 --> 00:50:48.300
Do you guys have anything directly to do

00:50:48.300 --> 00:50:50.390
with when people say that Russian bots

00:50:50.390 --> 00:50:53.810
were hacking the election
and manipulating news stories

00:50:53.810 --> 00:50:54.892
and all of these things?

00:50:54.892 --> 00:50:58.470
How much directly does that
fall under your purview?

00:50:58.470 --> 00:50:59.520
- It doesn't, actually.

00:50:59.520 --> 00:51:02.010
So we do follow, of course,
just in the popular press

00:51:02.010 --> 00:51:04.580
some of the articles and
commentary that go on about that,

00:51:04.580 --> 00:51:07.540
but that would be more within
the purview of other agencies.

00:51:07.540 --> 00:51:09.330
Now I will say in terms of cybersecurity,

00:51:09.330 --> 00:51:12.130
we do work with our other
federal partners very closely.

00:51:12.991 --> 00:51:13.830
The Department of Homeland Security

00:51:13.830 --> 00:51:15.430
in particular has an agency,

00:51:15.430 --> 00:51:18.390
the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure
Security Agency, CISA,

00:51:18.390 --> 00:51:20.470
and that's led by a
gentleman, Chris Krebs,

00:51:20.470 --> 00:51:23.010
who I work with very regularly.

00:51:23.010 --> 00:51:24.030
I just met with him last week

00:51:24.030 --> 00:51:25.500
on some of the 5G security issues.

00:51:25.500 --> 00:51:27.950
So to the extent that the
actual hacking of networks

00:51:27.950 --> 00:51:30.950
is involved, we do have a seat
at the table, so to speak.

00:51:30.950 --> 00:51:32.490
- Do you find that the other commissioners

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:33.330
that you have to work with

00:51:33.330 --> 00:51:37.750
basically have a similar
philosophical leaning?

00:51:37.750 --> 00:51:40.070
- I think so, generally speaking,
when it comes to security,

00:51:40.070 --> 00:51:41.010
we're all on the same page.

00:51:41.010 --> 00:51:44.690
We want these telecom
networks to be secure.

00:51:44.690 --> 00:51:46.640
We might have different
answers on how to get there,

00:51:46.640 --> 00:51:49.190
but ultimately, I think
we do share the same goal.

00:51:49.190 --> 00:51:50.610
In fact, this is one of the issues.

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:53.740
I tapped one of my Democratic colleagues

00:51:53.740 --> 00:51:56.720
to represent the agency at some
of these 5G security issues

00:51:56.720 --> 00:51:59.800
precisely because I
recognize that the security

00:51:59.800 --> 00:52:02.150
of these 5G networks is
not a Republican issue,

00:52:02.150 --> 00:52:04.000
not a Democratic issues.

00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:06.567
It may raise some eyebrows,
"Why are you tapping somebody

00:52:06.567 --> 00:52:07.480
"from the other party?"

00:52:07.480 --> 00:52:09.280
but I think it's important for Americans

00:52:09.280 --> 00:52:10.950
to speak with one voice on this issue,

00:52:10.950 --> 00:52:14.480
and I'm more than happy
to share the credit.

00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:17.240
If we get the ball across the goal line,

00:52:17.240 --> 00:52:18.600
that's great if he can do it.

00:52:18.600 --> 00:52:20.700
- Can you talk generally, I
think that's a good transition,

00:52:20.700 --> 00:52:23.760
to just like what is the
culture in Washington right now?

00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:25.370
Not even necessarily with your job,

00:52:25.370 --> 00:52:27.060
but I think everyone's feeling this thing

00:52:27.060 --> 00:52:29.700
that just the machine
kinda doesn't work anymore.

00:52:29.700 --> 00:52:31.710
Everyone does everything
on partisan lines.

00:52:31.710 --> 00:52:34.520
You just mentioned that
you try not to do that.

00:52:34.520 --> 00:52:36.130
But just what is the culture?

00:52:36.130 --> 00:52:38.590
As someone that lives
and works in Washington,

00:52:38.590 --> 00:52:43.590
you're part of a machine that
administrations come and go,

00:52:43.860 --> 00:52:45.260
you're still gonna be there,

00:52:46.130 --> 00:52:48.804
and people are always talking
about that sorta thing now,

00:52:48.804 --> 00:52:51.750
that the Washington
machine just always exists.

00:52:51.750 --> 00:52:53.050
Can you just talk about
the culture a little bit?

00:52:53.050 --> 00:52:54.570
- It's increasingly poisonous.

00:52:54.570 --> 00:52:58.330
I have found that there's
much more tribalism

00:52:58.330 --> 00:53:00.620
in Washington than ever before.

00:53:00.620 --> 00:53:01.970
I'm not sure if it's a cause or an effect

00:53:01.970 --> 00:53:04.190
of the tribalism we
see in our body politic

00:53:04.190 --> 00:53:06.360
generally speaking, but
I do find it distressing

00:53:06.360 --> 00:53:09.360
a lot of the times that
some of the blow back

00:53:09.360 --> 00:53:11.070
I'll get on Twitter has
nothing to do with me,

00:53:11.070 --> 00:53:13.647
it's just, "Oh, you're
part of the administration,

00:53:13.647 --> 00:53:17.820
"screw you," and it's that
kind of knee-jerk tribalism,

00:53:17.820 --> 00:53:19.720
and conversely on the other side, too.

00:53:21.150 --> 00:53:23.120
If you're supporting one of the candidates

00:53:23.120 --> 00:53:24.477
on the campaign trail, "Screw you,

00:53:24.477 --> 00:53:26.490
"I don't wanna have
anything to do with you."

00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:29.970
That kind of ethos has permeated
Washington to some extent.

00:53:29.970 --> 00:53:31.510
It's unfortunate because I do think

00:53:31.510 --> 00:53:33.480
that at the end of the day
there are certain things

00:53:33.480 --> 00:53:35.339
that we did have a consensus on.

00:53:35.339 --> 00:53:36.400
I mean, even the free market principle,

00:53:36.400 --> 00:53:38.990
it was not that long ago
that I think we had--

00:53:38.990 --> 00:53:40.500
- Is that the most shocking one to you?

00:53:40.500 --> 00:53:42.160
'Cause that one seemed so bizarre to me,

00:53:42.160 --> 00:53:44.900
that we're debating things that have led

00:53:44.900 --> 00:53:47.840
to all of the success that
we all live in right now,

00:53:47.840 --> 00:53:49.870
and we're suddenly just
tossing it up there

00:53:49.870 --> 00:53:51.240
as if these things should be debated.

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:53.730
- It would be a close second
I would say to free speech.

00:53:53.730 --> 00:53:55.320
That's one of the issues that I think

00:53:55.320 --> 00:53:57.870
I have often said it
distinguishes American democracy

00:53:57.870 --> 00:53:59.060
from virtually any other society

00:53:59.060 --> 00:54:01.300
in the world today or even in history.

00:54:01.300 --> 00:54:03.920
I always say that free speech
is not just the cold guarantee

00:54:03.920 --> 00:54:05.340
that's on the partron of the Constitution.

00:54:05.340 --> 00:54:08.400
It's also a culture that
emerged for many, many years

00:54:08.400 --> 00:54:09.763
with great sacrifice.

00:54:11.036 --> 00:54:12.410
If you have a view that
is opposed to mine,

00:54:12.410 --> 00:54:13.847
the tradition of course was,

00:54:13.847 --> 00:54:16.010
what's that saying that's
attributed to Voltaire?

00:54:16.010 --> 00:54:17.120
I may disagree with what you say,

00:54:17.120 --> 00:54:19.700
but I'll fight for your right to say it.

00:54:19.700 --> 00:54:21.360
Increasingly, we see that, no,

00:54:21.360 --> 00:54:23.310
people want to wall off the public square,

00:54:23.310 --> 00:54:26.410
in fact push people out of
the public square altogether

00:54:26.410 --> 00:54:28.210
if there's a dissimilarity of views.

00:54:29.090 --> 00:54:30.990
That is something that
is extremely dangerous

00:54:30.990 --> 00:54:31.870
to this country.

00:54:31.870 --> 00:54:33.890
- Have you thought of one
of the interesting ideas

00:54:33.890 --> 00:54:35.670
that I've heard related to just backing up

00:54:35.670 --> 00:54:37.640
to the platforms themselves

00:54:37.640 --> 00:54:39.490
is that since these are private companies,

00:54:39.490 --> 00:54:40.770
that it's almost like let them do

00:54:40.770 --> 00:54:42.610
whatever they want, basically,

00:54:42.610 --> 00:54:45.420
but that then the government
could actually get involved

00:54:45.420 --> 00:54:48.290
in creating a platform that would respect

00:54:48.290 --> 00:54:50.080
all of the laws of the United States,

00:54:50.080 --> 00:54:52.190
and that that would be an interesting use

00:54:52.190 --> 00:54:54.270
of government money, because it's like,

00:54:54.270 --> 00:54:56.620
oh, all right, Twitter
can do what they want,

00:54:56.620 --> 00:54:57.910
YouTube can do what they want,

00:54:57.910 --> 00:54:59.900
but what if the government
actually just had a platform

00:54:59.900 --> 00:55:01.330
that as long as you're abiding

00:55:01.330 --> 00:55:02.900
by the laws of the United States,

00:55:02.900 --> 00:55:04.980
basically you are allowed to be on there,

00:55:04.980 --> 00:55:05.813
something like that.

00:55:05.813 --> 00:55:07.240
- I'd have to think about it more deeply.

00:55:07.240 --> 00:55:08.460
I guess two of my concerns would be,

00:55:08.460 --> 00:55:11.340
number one, the government
generally speaking

00:55:11.340 --> 00:55:14.400
is not well-positioned to own and operate

00:55:14.400 --> 00:55:15.233
a platform like that.
- Be creating all of that.

00:55:15.233 --> 00:55:16.620
- And the second one would be,

00:55:16.620 --> 00:55:18.400
as we saw with the debates
over the Fairness Doctrine

00:55:18.400 --> 00:55:23.400
in the 1970s and 1980s, that
that doctrine was misnamed

00:55:23.920 --> 00:55:26.720
precisely because it
empowered government officials

00:55:26.720 --> 00:55:29.020
to decide, to essentially assign points

00:55:29.020 --> 00:55:31.420
based on what side of the
political ledger you were on.

00:55:31.420 --> 00:55:33.986
And that is the kind of thing that worry--

00:55:33.986 --> 00:55:34.860
- So basically it was
the unfairness doctrine.

00:55:34.860 --> 00:55:36.190
People think, well, it sounded right,

00:55:36.190 --> 00:55:37.410
I mean, it's what they do with everything,

00:55:37.410 --> 00:55:38.880
it sounds right, the Fairness Doctrine.

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:41.170
We're gonna have everybody's
ideas be out there equally,

00:55:41.170 --> 00:55:43.350
but at the end of the day,
if the market doesn't,

00:55:43.350 --> 00:55:45.350
if some people just don't
care about your ideas,

00:55:45.350 --> 00:55:47.190
why should the government
be propping you up,

00:55:47.190 --> 00:55:48.410
and that's what it was doing, right?

00:55:48.410 --> 00:55:50.140
- Yeah, that's always one of my concerns.

00:55:50.140 --> 00:55:52.470
The same thing when it
come to spending money.

00:55:52.470 --> 00:55:54.760
If somebody tells me, "Oh, this
service or product is free,"

00:55:54.760 --> 00:55:56.260
well, sometimes the only problem with free

00:55:56.260 --> 00:55:57.310
is that it costs too much

00:55:57.310 --> 00:55:58.880
and you have to always
look at the fine print.

00:55:58.880 --> 00:56:01.280
So too when it comes
to free speech issues.

00:56:01.280 --> 00:56:03.173
I do think that's one of the issues

00:56:03.173 --> 00:56:04.560
I have to think a little
more deeply about,

00:56:04.560 --> 00:56:06.540
but that would be my instant reaction.

00:56:06.540 --> 00:56:10.380
- So are you hopeful then that
our defense of free speech

00:56:10.380 --> 00:56:14.370
will continue and that
we're gonna maintain

00:56:14.370 --> 00:56:16.700
this rare First Amendment
thing that we've got,

00:56:16.700 --> 00:56:18.190
that is the jealousy of the world?

00:56:18.190 --> 00:56:21.160
- I don't know, so when I
travel around the country,

00:56:21.160 --> 00:56:23.590
and I've now been to all 48
states in the continental U.S.

00:56:23.590 --> 00:56:24.700
and Puerto Rico and the Virgin Islands,

00:56:24.700 --> 00:56:26.860
and you meet people who are just trying

00:56:26.860 --> 00:56:29.250
to put food on the table, work hard,

00:56:29.250 --> 00:56:30.783
and raise their families, you get a sense

00:56:30.783 --> 00:56:34.050
that that bedrock understanding
of America's commitment

00:56:34.050 --> 00:56:35.830
to free speech is still strong.

00:56:35.830 --> 00:56:37.130
But my god, when you see some of these

00:56:37.130 --> 00:56:39.460
controversies emerging
on college campuses,

00:56:39.460 --> 00:56:42.693
on internet platforms like
Twitter, it does make you worry,

00:56:43.666 --> 00:56:45.570
are we going to have a
generation of leaders

00:56:45.570 --> 00:56:48.540
that is committed to that
ideal, and I don't know.

00:56:48.540 --> 00:56:53.010
I mean, I hope so, and I think
it does rub a lot of people

00:56:53.010 --> 00:56:54.830
of good will on both sides the wrong way

00:56:54.830 --> 00:56:57.800
to know that there are
certain correct views

00:56:57.800 --> 00:57:00.300
and only those views should
be heard in the public square.

00:57:00.300 --> 00:57:04.220
Because one day your views
might be viewed as incorrect.

00:57:04.220 --> 00:57:05.361
- [Dave] I know the feeling.

00:57:05.361 --> 00:57:07.701
(laughing)

00:57:07.701 --> 00:57:09.250
- I don't know, I guess this might be

00:57:09.250 --> 00:57:11.770
the naive Kansan in me, but
I really do feel optimistic

00:57:11.770 --> 00:57:15.270
that that instinct that runs
deep in the American psyche

00:57:15.270 --> 00:57:16.440
is going to win out in the long run,

00:57:16.440 --> 00:57:18.090
but there is some very
determined opponents

00:57:18.090 --> 00:57:19.470
on the other side, to be sure.

00:57:19.470 --> 00:57:21.920
- So we talked about a couple
of the future things here.

00:57:21.920 --> 00:57:23.280
So we're building this 5G network,

00:57:23.280 --> 00:57:24.470
we talked about space a little bit,

00:57:24.470 --> 00:57:26.760
but just to wrap us up nicely,

00:57:26.760 --> 00:57:27.970
what else should we be thinking about?

00:57:27.970 --> 00:57:28.803
What are the other things

00:57:28.803 --> 00:57:30.880
that are on the horizon for you guys

00:57:30.880 --> 00:57:32.880
that maybe the average
person isn't thinking about,

00:57:32.880 --> 00:57:35.190
that if we started thinking
about it a little more clearly,

00:57:35.190 --> 00:57:37.700
we wouldn't be in the, oh,
net neutrality's going away,

00:57:37.700 --> 00:57:39.990
the world is gonna end tomorrow.

00:57:39.990 --> 00:57:42.145
So let's do a little
buffering for the future here.

00:57:42.145 --> 00:57:42.978
(laughing)

00:57:42.978 --> 00:57:43.811
- So the biggest thing we're--

00:57:43.811 --> 00:57:44.933
- And if you answer this right.

00:57:46.180 --> 00:57:47.060
- [Ajit] Ah, all right.

00:57:47.060 --> 00:57:48.010
- I'll give you a Reese's.

00:57:48.010 --> 00:57:50.140
I'll even put the Reese's back down there.

00:57:50.140 --> 00:57:51.640
- This sort of Pavlovian bribery

00:57:51.640 --> 00:57:53.149
is gonna get you nowhere, my friend.

00:57:53.149 --> 00:57:55.310
(laughing)

00:57:55.310 --> 00:57:57.250
No, I think in conjunction
with the great work

00:57:57.250 --> 00:57:59.790
we're doing on 5G and
closing the digital divide,

00:57:59.790 --> 00:58:02.570
the one thing I would flag for
people is how that interacts

00:58:02.570 --> 00:58:05.060
with artificial intelligence
and machine learning.

00:58:05.060 --> 00:58:07.130
The networks of the future are
going to be smart networks.

00:58:07.130 --> 00:58:09.570
It's not going to be a
person operating locally

00:58:09.570 --> 00:58:10.640
managing a network.

00:58:10.640 --> 00:58:13.440
It's going to be software
using algorithms to decide

00:58:13.440 --> 00:58:15.954
how best to optimize that network.

00:58:15.954 --> 00:58:16.787
One of the things we've been doing,

00:58:16.787 --> 00:58:18.680
we just held the first
ever forum in the FCC

00:58:18.680 --> 00:58:20.360
on artificial intelligence
and machine learning

00:58:20.360 --> 00:58:22.000
last December, and I've been working

00:58:22.000 --> 00:58:23.510
with a lot of entrepreneurs on this

00:58:23.510 --> 00:58:26.240
to try to figure out what is the future?

00:58:26.240 --> 00:58:27.630
Not just the automation of jobs,

00:58:27.630 --> 00:58:29.090
but how will it make us smarter?

00:58:29.090 --> 00:58:31.060
For example, if you're
talking about wireless sensors

00:58:31.060 --> 00:58:32.540
that patients might wear,

00:58:32.540 --> 00:58:34.070
even when they're not in the hospital,

00:58:34.070 --> 00:58:35.160
and those sensors could transmit

00:58:35.160 --> 00:58:37.830
at very low bandwidth
amounts of information,

00:58:37.830 --> 00:58:39.570
your vital signs and what not,

00:58:39.570 --> 00:58:42.520
an algorithm could analyze it very quickly

00:58:42.520 --> 00:58:45.150
to determine if a healthcare provider

00:58:45.150 --> 00:58:46.370
needs to intervene quicker.

00:58:46.370 --> 00:58:48.445
That's the kind of thing we're
looking at in the future.

00:58:48.445 --> 00:58:49.330
The same thing when it come to even things

00:58:49.330 --> 00:58:51.460
like precision agriculture,
believe it or not,

00:58:51.460 --> 00:58:53.490
incredibly connected farms
that are gonna require

00:58:53.490 --> 00:58:56.050
AI and machine learning
tools to figure out,

00:58:56.050 --> 00:58:57.370
buys in the square foot,

00:58:57.370 --> 00:58:58.720
where do we need to apply fertilizer

00:58:58.720 --> 00:59:00.810
and where don't we need
to apply fertilizer?

00:59:00.810 --> 00:59:02.990
Across all of these industries,
AI and machine learning

00:59:02.990 --> 00:59:05.520
is gonna have a huge
transformative effect.

00:59:05.520 --> 00:59:07.460
I don't know what the future is,

00:59:07.460 --> 00:59:09.920
but I do think it's important
for us to think about it.

00:59:09.920 --> 00:59:11.840
I didn't mean to gloss
over the automation factor.

00:59:11.840 --> 00:59:13.950
That's another thing as well
that I hear some concern about.

00:59:13.950 --> 00:59:15.520
If you're a long-haul trucker,

00:59:15.520 --> 00:59:18.290
or even a lawyer, or a pharmacist,

00:59:18.290 --> 00:59:21.120
increasingly those jobs
are getting disrupted,

00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:23.220
and I do think it's important
to have a conversation

00:59:23.220 --> 00:59:24.530
about the future of work.

00:59:24.530 --> 00:59:26.250
That's not necessarily
the FCC's bailiwick,

00:59:26.250 --> 00:59:27.980
but I do think it's a
larger social phenomenon

00:59:27.980 --> 00:59:30.220
that some people are worried about,

00:59:30.220 --> 00:59:31.560
some people are optimistic about,

00:59:31.560 --> 00:59:34.430
and I'm not sure where
the conversation will go,

00:59:34.430 --> 00:59:35.621
but it's important to have.

00:59:35.621 --> 00:59:36.454
- Since you made a Yoda reference before,

00:59:36.454 --> 00:59:37.450
I really thought you were gonna drop

00:59:37.450 --> 00:59:39.142
a "Skynet Terminator" reference there.

00:59:39.142 --> 00:59:40.943
(laughing)

00:59:40.943 --> 00:59:42.490
But I mean, really, that is it,

00:59:42.490 --> 00:59:45.630
like how much power will
we give to the machines,

00:59:45.630 --> 00:59:49.410
and does the day come when
suddenly they're in charge,

00:59:49.410 --> 00:59:50.517
and then they go, "Huh, well,

00:59:50.517 --> 00:59:52.150
"the humans should
probably have less power."

00:59:52.150 --> 00:59:53.850
- Yeah, I was joking with one
of my friends the other day.

00:59:53.850 --> 00:59:55.832
I don't know if you saw "Wall-E"

00:59:55.832 --> 00:59:57.170
way back when.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:59:57.170 --> 00:59:58.260
- Is it gonna be like "Wall-E"

00:59:58.260 --> 01:00:00.237
or the "Matrix," the worse case.

01:00:00.237 --> 01:00:01.787
"Wall-E" where they do
all the work for us,

01:00:01.787 --> 01:00:04.993
"Matrix" we're essentially
batteries for everybody else.

01:00:04.993 --> 01:00:06.610
- I mean, I'm pretty
sure we're in some mix

01:00:06.610 --> 01:00:07.900
of both of those right now.

01:00:07.900 --> 01:00:09.799
- It's turtles all the
way down, my friends.

01:00:09.799 --> 01:00:10.860
(laughing)

01:00:10.860 --> 01:00:12.040
- It's been a pleasure chatting with you.

01:00:12.040 --> 01:00:12.873
Thanks for coming in.

01:00:12.873 --> 01:00:15.410
And you guys can follow
Chairman Pai on Twitter.

01:00:15.410 --> 01:00:18.360
Tell him everything that you
think about net neutrality,

01:00:18.360 --> 01:00:19.480
the good, bad, and the ugly.

01:00:19.480 --> 01:00:20.590
I apologize for that.

01:00:20.590 --> 01:00:21.457
(Ajit laughing)

